      <section id="abstract">
        <div class="abstract">
          <p class="abstract__title">Abstract</p>
          <p class="abstract__lede"><strong>In one sentence:</strong> when you exercise discretionary influence over the conditions under which others live, choose and develop, and when they cannot exit or contest that influence effectively, you owe them a proportionate account of it — and you cannot be the sole judge of whether that account is good enough.</p>
          <p><strong>What this essay is, and what it is not.</strong> It is a framework for deciding when influence creates a duty to answer, how demanding that duty is, who must discharge it, and how it can be discharged without turning into new domination. It is not a new foundational principle in the tradition of Pettit, Forst or Jonas. Its central principle — the Answerable Influence Principle (AIP) — is a <em>thin, proportionate, abductively supported</em> synthesis of existing traditions, not a demonstration of necessity. Its contribution is partly operational — the activation rule, the duty ladder with safeguard analysis, the rating protocol, the illustration of how ECP may supplement Young in a concrete case, the measurable indicators of symbiosis, the six-step conflict structure with residual loss, and the conversion of the reviewer regress from diagnosis to design — and partly philosophical: the <em>epistemic burden principle</em> (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>), which argues that asymmetric influence creates a heightened burden of epistemic justification.</p>
          <p><strong>1. Three questions.</strong> The framework distinguishes three questions that are often run together: <em>responsibility</em> (who shaped the conditions), <em>answerability</em> (who must explain and submit to correction), and <em>justice</em> (what is substantively permissible). It answers the first two and leaves the third to substantive theories of justice (<a href="#s1-2">§1.2</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>2. Ground.</strong> AIP is a proportionate principle: an actor that exercises consequential discretionary influence over conditions on which another party significantly depends, and that party lacks effective means to prevent, exit or correct the influence, owes proportionate answerability to those affected. Four considerations support it — authorship, dependence, non-imposition, remedial — which together make AIP plausible without proving it necessary. AIP is primarily epistemic: it protects against the actor confusing its own view with the truth. It is <em>abductively</em> supported and has stated defeaters D1–D4 (<a href="#s2-5">§2.5</a>). It is motivated by a fallibilist premise and a stated bridge norm (<a href="#s2">§2.0</a>), not derived from them. The framework handles domination; it does not solve it.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Standing and symbiosis.</strong> Who is owed an account, including beings that cannot speak for themselves, handled through four tiers of standing under uncertainty with an explicit plausibility threshold (<a href="#s4">§4</a>). Symbiosis is specified with observable indicators (S1–S3), so that the framework's ideal is as contestable as its procedure (<a href="#s5-1">§5.1</a>). Collective contestation may represent silent parties, but it must not undermine individual contestation (§4.6 vii). Section <a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a> develops a more limited claim: asymmetric influence creates a heightened burden to seek correction from sources not controlled by the decision-maker.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Activation.</strong> One rule: a discretion gate plus three drivers (entrenchment, severity, contestation deficit) that determine one of three levels of duty, with anchors and a rating protocol that make the judgments visible and contestable (<a href="#s6">§6</a>–<a href="#s9">9</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>5. Structure.</strong> The three safeguards — exit, review, justification — are complementary, not interchangeable (<a href="#s8-1">§8.1</a>). A floor of minimum conditions, the prohibition on being sole judge of one's own influence, the reviewer regress treated as a design problem, a six-step conflict structure with residual loss, and accounts of future generations and learning (<a href="#s10">§10</a>–<a href="#s13">13</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>6. Application and testing.</strong> Worked cases including one that appears to refute the framework (<a href="#s14-2b">§14.2b</a>), five proposed evaluation conditions for the activation rule, with independent measures where possible, provisional thresholds and preregistration requirements (<a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a>), and modular conditions for replacement (<a href="#s15-3">§15.3</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>Answerability is the procedure. Symbiosis is the ideal it serves.</strong> Relations of dependence in which the dependence is not exploited, difference is not forced into sameness, and the dependent party keeps the conditions for continued existence, development and participation — that is what the framework aims at. The relation between procedure and ideal is more than instrumental: where one party controls the terms of dependence, the quality of its own assessment is not enough.</p>
          <p>The framework has not been empirically tested. Its activation rule is specified so that it can be (<a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a>); its other parts are argued and offered for contestation.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <aside class="preface" id="preface" aria-label="The argument in brief">
        <h2>The argument in brief</h2>
        <p>The essay is long, and its spine can be stated in a page. This is that page.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>The problem.</strong> Influence that is structural, persistent, diffuse and hard to attribute to any single wrongful act is not adequately covered by existing moral vocabulary — blame, rights, consent, regulation (<a href="#s1">§1</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>The distinction.</strong> Three questions must not be collapsed: responsibility (who shaped the conditions), answerability (who must explain and submit to correction), and justice (what is substantively permissible). The framework answers the first two (<a href="#s1-2">§1.2</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>The principle (AIP).</strong> An actor that exercises consequential discretionary influence over conditions on which another party significantly depends, and that party lacks effective means to prevent, exit or correct the influence, owes proportionate answerability to those affected (<a href="#s2-2">§2.2</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Why AIP.</strong> Four considerations — authorship, dependence, non-imposition, remedial — support it. AIP is <em>abductively</em> supported, not demonstrated, and has stated defeaters D1–D4 (<a href="#s2-5">§2.5</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>The trigger.</strong> Influence creates the duty when discretion is present and exit or review is unavailable or ineffective. How much duty scales with entrenchment, severity and contestation deficit (<a href="#s7">§7</a>–<a href="#s8">§8</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>The epistemic claim.</strong> Under asymmetric influence, the more powerful party has weaker grounds for treating its own assessment as decisive. It therefore bears a heightened burden to obtain independent evidence and correction from those affected (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>The limits.</strong> AIP is not necessary. It is defensible. The framework <em>handles</em> domination; it does not <em>solve</em> it. None of this is a proof of anything. It is an invitation to argue.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="preface-note">If you read only one section after this page, read <a href="#s7">§7</a> — that is where the framework either works or does not. If you read only one passage, read <a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a> — that is where it makes its main epistemic proposal.</p>
      </aside>

