        <h3>Five questions the framework addresses</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>When does influence create duty?</strong> <a href="#s7">§7</a> gives the activation rule — a discretion gate plus three drivers.</li>
          <li><strong>How much duty, and what kind?</strong> <a href="#s8">§8</a> gives a three-level ladder from explanation, through review and safeguard analysis, to independent authorisation with safeguard mix.</li>
          <li><strong>Whose interests count?</strong> <a href="#s4">§4</a> gives four tiers of standing under uncertainty; <a href="#s10">§10</a> gives five minimum conditions in relationships of dependence.</li>
          <li><strong>Who watches the powerful?</strong> No actor should be the sole judge of its own influence (<a href="#s11">§11</a>). <a href="#s11-4">§11.4</a> converts the regress into a design constraint.</li>
          <li><strong>When should the framework itself be abandoned — or replaced?</strong> <a href="#s2-5">§2.5</a> states the defeaters for AIP; <a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a> states the defeaters for the epistemic burden principle; <a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a> gives the failure conditions for the activation rule; <a href="#s15-3">§15.3</a> gives replacement conditions for the framework as a whole.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>What the framework does not do</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>It does not tell you what to decide. It tells you what questions you owe an answer to — and to whom.</li>
          <li>It does not claim neutrality. It takes a substantive moral position (<a href="#s2">§2</a>) and defends it against rivals.</li>
          <li>It does not eliminate the problem of power (<a href="#s11-4">§11.4</a>, <a href="#s2-6">§2.6</a>). It converts the regress into a design constraint.</li>
          <li>It does not claim universality for AIP. It claims that four considerations point toward it (<a href="#s2-1">§2.1</a>), and that this is evidence but not proof.</li>
          <li>It does not claim that AIP is necessary. It claims that AIP is <em>abductively</em> defensible, and that it has stated defeaters.</li>
          <li>It does not solve domination. It handles it (<a href="#s3-2">§3.2</a>, <a href="#s16">§16</a>).</li>
          <li>It does not claim that its epistemic argument is proven. It offers a limited proposal about the burden of justification under asymmetric influence, open to counterexamples (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>).</li>
        </ul>

