        <p class="part-divider__label">Part I</p>
        <p class="part-divider__title">Problem, Scope and Ground</p>
      </div>

      <section id="s1">
        <h2><span class="num">1.</span>The Problem, Scope and Terms</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">The capacity to shape other people's conditions has grown faster than the practices for answering for it.</p>

        <p>Recommendation systems, credit scoring, clinical triage tools and AI assistants shape the conditions of millions. So do states writing policy and companies setting terms, and, at a different scale, clinicians, teachers, landlords and parents. Existing moral vocabulary covers parts of this territory: blame for wrongful acts, rights for protected interests, consent for voluntary exchanges, regulation for what law has already caught. What falls between them is influence that is structural, persistent, diffuse and hard to attribute to any single wrongful act, and that nonetheless determines what others can do and become.</p>

        <h3 id="s1-1">1.1 What the framework does and does not do</h3>
        <p>It does not tell anyone what to decide. It is not a decision theory, and it is not a substitute for law, rights or substantive standards of justice. It is meant to be used alongside them, as an account of what actors owe to those they affect, and to whom.</p>

        <h3 id="s1-2">1.2 Three questions that must not be collapsed</h3>
        <p>The framework distinguishes three questions that are often run together:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Responsibility.</strong> Who has contributed to shaping the relevant conditions, and in what way?</li>
          <li><strong>Answerability.</strong> Who must explain, preserve evidence, respond to objections, and submit to correction?</li>
          <li><strong>Justice.</strong> Which outcomes, distributions, rights and protections are substantively permissible?</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The Answerable Influence Principle (<a href="#s2-2">§2.2</a>) addresses the first question and helps allocate the second. The Exercised-Capacity Principle (<a href="#s3">§3</a>) profiles actual discretionary influence. The Epistemic Burden Principle (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>) explains why those who exercise asymmetric influence must not rely exclusively on evidence they themselves control. The framework does <em>not</em> answer the third question. That is a substantive question for justice, and the framework can only specify what must be answered and to whom.</p>

        <h3 id="s1-3">1.3 Scope conditions: when the framework does not apply</h3>
        <p>The framework is offered for a specific class of cases. It does not claim to apply everywhere, and it is important to say where it does not.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Symmetrical relations.</strong> Where parties have roughly equal capacity to exit, review and contest, the framework adds little to ordinary practices of mutual justification among equals.</li>
          <li><strong>Relations governed by enforceable rights and effective regulation.</strong> Where law provides effective rights, review and remedies, the framework may add little. Formal legality alone is not enough: rights may be inaccessible, remedies delayed, or review structurally dependent on the actor. The framework supplements legal standards where those protections fail in practice; it never displaces them.</li>
          <li><strong>Purely causal influence without discretion.</strong> A person who is swept away by a flood does not owe an account to those downstream. The framework's trigger is <em>exercised or maintained discretionary influence</em>, not mere causal connection.</li>
          <li><strong>Trivial influence.</strong> A café changing its menu, a neighbour choosing a different route to work — these are not the cases the framework addresses. The severity floor in <a href="#s7">§7</a> is meant to exclude them.</li>
          <li><strong>Relations where the affected party is not a candidate for standing.</strong> Stones and rivers as such do not have standing under this framework (<a href="#s4-1">§4.1</a>). The framework marks the edge of its claims.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>These scope conditions are not decorative. They are part of the framework's claim to be a usable tool rather than a totalising theory.</p>

        <h3 id="s1-4">1.4 Terms</h3>
        <p><strong>Influence:</strong> the exercise or maintenance of control over conditions under which others act, choose, develop or fare. Maintaining a structure one could alter, such as keeping a known-flawed system running, counts as influence. Mere ability to help does not (<a href="#s3">§3</a>).</p>
        <p><strong>Actor:</strong> a person, organisation or institution, including those that design, deploy and maintain technical systems. Technical systems are treated as instruments, and answerability falls on the humans and organisations behind them (whether some systems may themselves be affected parties is taken up in <a href="#s4">§4</a>).</p>
        <p><strong>Affected party:</strong> any being whose interests are materially affected by the influence.</p>
        <p><strong>Discretion:</strong> the actor has genuine alternatives at tolerable cost and a role in setting the influence, rather than merely executing a rule it had no part in making or contesting.</p>
        <p><strong>Answerability:</strong> a duty to give reasons for the conditions one helps create or maintain, to those affected or their representatives; to permit examination of those reasons; and to remain open to correction. It is distinct from <em>blame</em>, from <em>liability</em> and from <em>authority</em>. Under the epistemic burden principle (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>), it is also the heightened epistemic safeguard for the more powerful party to know whether symbiosis has been achieved.</p>

        <h3 id="s1-5">1.5 Relation to existing work</h3>
        <p>The framework is a synthesis and an operationalisation, and its intellectual debts are substantial: Pettit and Lovett on domination, Forst on the right to justification, Scanlon on reasonable rejection, Young on structural responsibility, Bovens and Shoemaker on accountability and answerability, Tronto, Held and Goodin on care and vulnerability. <strong>Its contribution is comparative and operational, with a limited philosophical proposal rather than a new foundational principle.</strong> What it offers is (i) a proportionate principle for the exercise of discretion, (ii) an allocation of duties by capacity, (iii) an explicit rating protocol, (iv) a proposed operational supplement to the social connection model (<a href="#s3-2">§3.2</a>), (v) stated defeaters for AIP (<a href="#s2-5">§2.5</a>), stated defeaters for the epistemic burden principle (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>), and stated failure and replacement conditions for the activation rule (<a href="#s15">§15</a>), and (vi) the epistemic burden principle itself (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>), which is the framework's positive philosophical contribution. Whether the combination earns its keep is for readers to judge.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>The framework answers three questions: <em>who</em> has shaped the relevant conditions, <em>who</em> must answer for them, and <em>to whom</em>. It does not answer the fourth question — what is substantively just.</li>
            <li>It has explicit scope conditions: symmetrical relations, relations governed by enforceable rights, purely causal influence without discretion, trivial influence, and relations where the affected party is not a candidate for standing are not its target.</li>
            <li>Its contribution is comparative and partly philosophical: an explicit, graduated account of the evidential burden that accompanies asymmetric discretionary influence.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="s2">
        <h2><span class="num">2.</span>The Ground of Responsibility</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Influence is a fact; obligation is a normative claim. The framework must say openly which considerations turn the first into the second.</p>

        <h3 id="s2-0">2.0 A motivating note: fallibilism and the burden of judgment</h3>
        <div class="key-prop" data-label="Motivation (F) and bridge norm (B)">
          <p><strong>F (fallibilism).</strong> No actor fully understands the conditions it shapes, or everything that depends on what it does.</p>
          <p><strong>B (bridge norm).</strong> Where an actor's influence falls on others who bear the cost of its errors, and who may see what the actor cannot, the actor should not be the sole judge of that influence; it should seek correction from them.</p>
        </div>

        <p><strong>What F and B do.</strong> F alone yields no duty. A fact about limited understanding does not by itself entail a norm, which is why B is stated openly as a normative premise rather than smuggled in. Together, F and B <em>motivate</em> the framework's central claim (<a href="#s2-2">§2.2</a>) and its epistemic burden principle (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>): they explain why answerability is a sensible remedy when understanding is incomplete. They do not <em>entail</em> the central claim. What follows is the argument proper.</p>

        <p><strong>What F does not claim.</strong> It does not claim that understanding comes in a hierarchy with levels above and below, that the more capable are entitled to rule, or that stones or other non-subjects have standing (<a href="#s4">§4</a>). Its one lesson for beings that fall outside the framework's threshold is epistemic: an actor's failure to see what something contributes is not evidence that it contributes nothing.</p>

        <p><strong>What would refute F as motivation.</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>F1.</strong> There is an actor or domain whose understanding of the conditions it shapes is in fact complete.</li>
          <li><strong>F2.</strong> The incompleteness is contingent and the actor alone can reliably remedy it.</li>
          <li><strong>F3.</strong> Correction by those affected does not, on balance, improve the actor's account or outcomes.</li>
          <li><strong>F4.</strong> The central claim and §5.2 are as well supported without F as with it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The claim made here is deliberately small: F gives a shared motivation, nothing more, and the framework is no weaker if a reader prefers to omit it.</p>

        <h3 id="s2-1">2.1 Why influence creates duties: four considerations</h3>
        <p>The framework cannot derive a moral obligation from the mere observation that one actor influences another. The missing step must be stated openly. The proposed argument rests on four connected considerations, which together support the Answerable Influence Principle.</p>

        <h4 id="s2-1-1">2.1.1 The authorship consideration</h4>
        <p>Where an actor exercises meaningful discretion over conditions that structure another party's available choices, the actor is not merely observing those conditions. It is helping determine the practical environment within which another party must act.</p>
        <p>This does not make the actor responsible for everything that happens within that environment. Nor does it make the actor the sole author of the affected party's circumstances. It does, however, distinguish the actor from a bystander. The greater the actor's discretionary contribution to the conditions, the stronger the reason to require an account of how those conditions are shaped.</p>

        <h4 id="s2-1-2">2.1.2 The dependence consideration</h4>
        <p>Influence becomes morally more significant when another party depends on the conditions being shaped. Dependence alone does not establish wrongdoing — a child depends on caregivers, patients on medical institutions, societies on infrastructure, ecosystems on conditions maintained by many interacting processes. The relevant question is not whether dependence exists, but whether one party can substantially alter the dependent party's conditions while the latter lacks effective means to protect or correct itself.</p>
        <p>Where this asymmetry exists, the influential actor has a special responsibility not to treat the dependent party's inability to resist as permission.</p>

        <h4 id="s2-1-3">2.1.3 The non-imposition consideration</h4>
        <p>A person or institution cannot reasonably claim that its own interests, interpretations or priorities automatically settle the conditions under which others must live. The reason is not that every affected party is equally informed or always correct. It is that the actor's control over the conditions does not itself establish the legitimacy of the actor's judgment about those conditions.</p>
        <p>Where an actor imposes consequential arrangements on others, it owes reasons proportionate to the extent and character of that imposition. This is a limited principle of reciprocity: those who exercise discretionary power over others must be prepared to expose the relevant exercise of power to standards that do not depend solely on their own approval.</p>

        <h4 id="s2-1-4">2.1.4 The remedial consideration</h4>
        <p>Responsibility is not only retrospective. It can also arise from an actor's present capacity to prevent, correct or reduce consequences that it has helped produce or continues to maintain. An actor may inherit an unjust arrangement without having created it. Historical blame may then be misplaced, but prospective responsibility may still arise when the actor has substantial discretion and a reasonable capacity to improve the arrangement. This distinction prevents responsibility from becoming identical to guilt.</p>

        <h3 id="s2-2">2.2 The Answerable Influence Principle (AIP)</h3>
        <div class="key-prop" data-label="The Answerable Influence Principle">
          <p>An actor that exercises consequential discretionary influence over conditions on which another party significantly depends, and that party lacks effective means to prevent, exit or correct the influence, owes proportionate answerability to those affected.</p>
        </div>
        <p>The duty increases with: the extent of discretionary control; the significance of the conditions affected; the degree and duration of dependence; the foreseeability of consequences; the difficulty of reversal; the affected party's lack of effective correction; and the actor's capacity to respond.</p>
        <p>This principle does not establish that every influence is wrongful. It establishes that certain forms of influence require an account, and that the account must be proportionate to what is at stake. A minor and reversible decision does not justify the same demands as a decision that substantially determines another party's future possibilities.</p>

        <h3 id="s2-3">2.3 What AIP is not</h3>
        <p>AIP is not a claim that all influence is wrongful, or that every dependence is domination. It is not a decision procedure and it does not settle substantive questions of justice. It is a principle for identifying when an actor owes an account, and how demanding that account is — not what the actor must decide.</p>
        <p>AIP is primarily an <em>epistemic</em> principle. It is not a claim that answerability <em>makes</em> actions right. It is a claim that actors who cannot be contested — whether by exit, review or justification — are more likely to be wrong, and that the practice of contestation is the best available safeguard against this. The epistemic framing is the old principle that no one should be judge in their own cause, applied to influence. It is compatible with moral realism and with consequentialism, because both can accept contestation as an epistemic safeguard without accepting it as a fundamental value.</p>

        <h3 id="s2-4">2.4 The abductive support</h3>
        <p>AIP is offered as a plausible explanation of a set of recurring risks associated with unaccountable power: that actors with unaccountable discretion have incentives and opportunities to overestimate their benevolence and understanding of affected parties; that exit, review and contestation can expose errors that internal assessment may miss; that errors may cluster around the actor's own interests and categories, so that the more powerful party's categories become the default and the less powerful party's perspective is treated as a deviation. Whether these patterns are prevalent and causal is an empirical question. AIP is one compact principle that organises the considerations; it is not the only principle that could do so, and its attraction is that it accommodates several safeguards without requiring a single substantive theory of the good.</p>

        <h3 id="s2-5">2.5 Stated defeaters for AIP</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">A principle that cannot be defeated is not a principle; it is a dogma. AIP is offered with its defeaters stated in advance.</p>
        <p>If any of the following holds, AIP should be rejected or substantially revised.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>D1 — The unaccountable-power hypothesis is false.</strong> If it can be shown empirically that actors with unaccountable discretion over others' conditions do <em>not</em> systematically produce worse outcomes — measured independently of the actors' own reports — than actors subject to exit, review or contestation, then AIP's epistemic justification collapses.</li>
          <li><strong>D2 — A better principle explains the same cases.</strong> If a rival principle (a non-proportionate version, a purely consequentialist version, a purely rights-based version) explains all the cases AIP explains, more simply, and generates clearer practical guidance, AIP should be replaced.</li>
          <li><strong>D3 — The four considerations collapse into one.</strong> If it can be shown that authorship, dependence, non-imposition and remedial responsibility are not independent considerations but one tradition dressed in four vocabularies, the argument for AIP reduces to a single route and its support is much weaker.</li>
          <li><strong>D4 — The costs exceed the benefits.</strong> If adopting AIP produces worse outcomes for the affected than not adopting it — measured by independent indicators, not by the framework's own rating protocol — AIP should be abandoned, whatever its theoretical merits.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 id="s2-6">2.6 The power objection</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">A procedural framework that tells the powerful how to justify themselves may simply give them a new vocabulary for doing what they were going to do anyway. This is the most serious objection to any procedural framework, and it deserves its own section.</p>

        <p><strong>The objection.</strong> The framework asks powerful actors to explain, to submit to review, to justify. But the powerful are precisely the actors with the resources to perform these rituals convincingly. They can hire the best reviewers, publish the most polished justifications, and select the indicators that make them look good. Proceduralism, on this objection, does not constrain power. It <em>legitimates</em> power by giving it a veneer of accountability.</p>

        <p><strong>The reply, in three parts.</strong></p>
        <p><em>First, the objection is partly correct, and the framework should say so more clearly than it sometimes does.</em> Proceduralism <em>can</em> legitimate power. That is why <a href="#s11-4">§11.4</a> treats T5 as a precondition rather than a diagnosis. The framework's response to the objection is not to deny it but to build the denial into its design.</p>
        <p><em>Second, the objection is not specific to proceduralism.</em> Any normative framework can be captured by the powerful. The question is not whether a framework can be captured, but whether it has internal resources against capture that its rivals lack. T5-as-precondition is such a resource: it does not prevent capture, but it makes the capture visible, and it downgrades the legitimacy of a captured process.</p>
        <p><em>Third, the framework's modesty is not an admission of failure; it is a claim about what any framework can do.</em> A framework that claimed to <em>solve</em> the problem of power would be either naive or dangerous. The framework claims to <em>handle</em> it — to convert the problem from an unstructured condition into a set of design constraints that can be observed, contested and revised.</p>

        <p><strong>What this does not do.</strong> It does not answer the objection fully. A sufficiently powerful actor can capture the publication requirement itself. What the framework can do is make the capture visible, and visible capture is the precondition of contestation. Whether visibility is enough is a political question, not a philosophical one. The objection is the second-hardest the framework faces, after the realist objection in <a href="#objections">objection 1</a>.</p>

        <h3 id="s2-7">2.7 Rivals, taken in their best forms</h3>
        <p><strong>Consequentialism.</strong> Sophisticated consequentialists can accept AIP as an institutional implication, especially in its proportionate form. The real disagreement is whether AIP is basic or derived, and it matters where aggregate welfare seems to favour overriding contestation. There the framework sides with contestation in severe cases, for epistemic reasons, while permitting temporary override in emergencies subject to sunset and review (<a href="#s12-3">§12.3</a>).</p>
        <p><strong>Kantianism.</strong> It shares the core but grounds standing in rational agency, which creates problems for infants, the cognitively disabled and animals. <a href="#s4">§4</a> handles these through tiers.</p>
        <p><strong>Virtue ethics.</strong> It supports the humility the framework requires, but it is weak on structural questions: who reviews whom, and by what procedure.</p>
        <p><strong>Justice as fairness and capabilities.</strong> Rawls and the capabilities approach (Sen 2009; Nussbaum 2006) supply substantive accounts of what is at stake for people. The framework is procedural and can draw on these to calibrate severity (<a href="#s9">§9</a>). The cost is stated in <a href="#s2-9">§2.9</a>.</p>
        <p><strong>Rights.</strong> Rights are outputs, and the framework treats them as external constraints. What it adds is an account of when and how actors must justify influence in the space that rights and regulation leave open.</p>
        <p><strong>Realism.</strong> The realist who denies that power owes anything to the weak cannot be refuted, only outlasted. The framework's reply is that the realist who <em>argues</em> for realism already relies on the practice of argument, which presupposes something like AIP. This reply is developed in <a href="#objections">objection 1</a>.</p>

        <h3 id="s2-8">2.8 Traditions that start elsewhere</h3>
        <p>Ubuntu-inspired moral theory (Metz 2007), Confucian role ethics (Ames 2011) and many Indigenous relational ontologies (Watts 2013; de la Cadena 2015; Kimmerer 2013) begin from relational personhood and obligations to kin, community and land. They tend to arrive at related demands on those who hold power over dependents, by different grounds. The framework treats them as additional considerations pointing toward its operative core — not as independent proofs. They also press two challenges: that the affected party may be a community rather than an individual, so that contestation and representation may be collective (<a href="#s4-6">§4.6</a>, <a href="#s10">§10</a>); and that the boundary of who counts is not settled by any one tradition (<a href="#s4">§4</a>).</p>

        <h3 id="s2-9">2.9 Costs</h3>
        <p>AIP is thin, so it underdetermines outcomes. The framework presupposes a minimal commitment to the equal standing of those affected; it is therefore not neutral among all comprehensive doctrines. Its aim is breadth of acceptability rather than neutrality. And because the support is abductive rather than demonstrative, it is open to the objection that the abduction is weak. The reply is the modular design of <a href="#s15-3">§15.3</a>: the ground can be replaced without discarding the rest, provided the replacement does the same work.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>AIP is the framework's central normative principle: consequential discretionary influence over another's conditions, combined with that party's lack of effective exit or correction, generates a proportionate duty of answerability.</li>
            <li>Four considerations support AIP: authorship, dependence, non-imposition, remedial. They are considerations, not independent proofs.</li>
            <li>AIP is primarily epistemic; it protects against the actor confusing its own view with the truth.</li>
            <li>Four defeaters (D1–D4) are stated in advance.</li>
            <li>The power objection (<a href="#s2-6">§2.6</a>) is the second-hardest the framework faces, after the realist objection.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="s3">
        <h2><span class="num">3.</span>The Exercised-Capacity Principle</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Responsibility should attend to the consequential discretionary influence an actor actually exercises or maintains — not merely to power possessed, harm caused or role occupied.</p>

        <div class="key-prop" data-label="The Exercised-Capacity Principle">
          <p>An actor's responsibility for shaping another party's conditions should be assessed in proportion to its actual discretionary contribution to those conditions, taking account of the consequences, foreseeability, available alternatives, role and capacity to respond.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Five elements are relevant:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Actual influence.</strong> What conditions does the actor meaningfully shape, maintain or control?</li>
          <li><strong>Discretion.</strong> How much room does the actor have to choose differently?</li>
          <li><strong>Consequence.</strong> How significant are the effects for the affected party?</li>
          <li><strong>Foreseeability.</strong> What could the actor reasonably have anticipated?</li>
          <li><strong>Response capacity.</strong> What can the actor reasonably do to prevent, explain or correct the consequences?</li>
        </ul>
        <p>These elements should not be collapsed into a single numerical score. They form a profile of responsibility.</p>

        <h3 id="s3-1">3.1 Why exercised capacity matters</h3>
        <p>Possessing a capacity does not necessarily mean exercising it. A person may have substantial technical ability but no meaningful influence over another party's circumstances. Conversely, an institution may exercise considerable control through routine procedures without possessing exceptional intelligence or individual intention. The relevant responsibility concerns the influence actually exercised, not an imagined hierarchy of capability.</p>
        <p>This also means that responsibility can be distributed. Several actors may exercise different forms of influence over the same conditions. Their duties should reflect their respective contributions and capacities rather than being assigned exclusively to the most visible actor.</p>
        <p>ECP separates itself from the demanding duty of rescue (Singer 1972) and from the overdemandingness that critics press against capacity-based views (Murphy 2000) by its trigger: exercised or maintained influence, combined with the unavailability or ineffectiveness of exit and review. Mere ability to help does not trigger it.</p>

        <h3 id="s3-2">3.2 ECP and the social connection model</h3>
        <p>Iris Marion Young's social connection model already provides an important account of responsibility for structural injustice (Young 2011). It avoids reducing responsibility to direct causation or individual blame and recognises that people participate in processes that reproduce unjust conditions.</p>
        <p>ECP should not claim to discover that structural responsibility exists. Its proposed contribution is narrower. Young's model offers a broad account of how responsibility arises from participation in structural processes. ECP attempts to specify a more operational question: when does an actor's particular exercise of discretion over another party's conditions generate a heightened duty of answerability, and how should that duty be graduated?</p>
        <p>The distinction is not that Young ignores power or that ECP replaces structural responsibility. Rather:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>structural connection helps identify participation in processes;</li>
          <li>ECP profiles actual discretionary influence within those processes;</li>
          <li>AIP explains why certain profiles generate special duties;</li>
          <li>the duty ladder (<a href="#s8">§8</a>) translates those duties into proportionate requirements.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>ECP is therefore a proposed allocation and operationalisation tool, not a demonstrated replacement for Young's theory. Its value depends on whether it produces more accurate and defensible allocations in cases where general structural responsibility does not sufficiently distinguish the roles of different actors.</p>

        <h3 id="s3-3">3.3 What ECP does not claim</h3>
        <p>ECP does not claim to have refuted Young. If Young's model can be given an allocation that does the same work without a discretionary trigger, ECP's advantage disappears. That is a live question, and the framework invites it.</p>
        <p>ECP also does not claim to be the only defensible allocation principle. A rival that produces more defensible allocations on a substantial set of cases should replace it (<a href="#s15-3">§15.3</a>).</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>ECP allocates responsibility by exercised discretionary influence, not by power possessed or harm caused alone.</li>
            <li>Five elements form a profile, not a score: actual influence, discretion, consequence, foreseeability, response capacity.</li>
            <li>ECP is a proposed allocation tool, not a replacement for Young's social connection model.</li>
            <li>The claim is comparative and operational, open to test and replacement.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>
