<section id="s4">
  <h2><span class="num">4.</span>Standing Under Uncertainty</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">Incomprehensibility is not evidence of unimportance, but neither is it evidence of importance. The framework needs a threshold.</p>

  <h3 id="s4-1">4.1 Four tiers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tier A: agents.</strong> Beings with normative agency (persons).</li>
    <li><strong>Tier B: welfare subjects.</strong> Beings with interests of their own: sentient animals, and humans who lack agency, such as infants.</li>
    <li><strong>Tier C: uncertain candidates.</strong> Entities for which there is a non-negligible, evidence-based probability of Tier B status (Birch 2024).</li>
    <li><strong>Tier D: non-subjects.</strong> Entities with no credible interests of their own: stones, rivers and ecosystems as such. The framework claims no direct standing for them. They matter through the interests of beings in Tiers A–C who depend on them, through future generations (<a href="#s12">§12</a>), and through values that people hold non-instrumentally.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="s4-2">4.2 The plausibility threshold</h3>
  <p>The test for Tier C is whether there is a credible, evidence-based reason to think the entity has states that matter to it: behavioural and physiological indicators of valenced experience, evolutionary or functional continuity with known subjects, or features associated with serious theories of consciousness. Without such a threshold, precaution generates reasons everywhere and guides nowhere (Sunstein 2005).</p>

  <h3 id="s4-3">4.3 The stone, restated</h3>
  <p>The stone marks the edge of the framework's claims. For beings that meet the threshold, our failure to understand their way of being is no reason to dismiss their interests. For the stone, which does not meet it, the framework claims no standing and says so.</p>

  <h3 id="s4-4">4.4 Artificial systems</h3>
  <p>Answerability for the influence of AI systems falls on the humans and organisations that design, deploy and maintain them. Whether some artificial systems may themselves be Tier C candidates is contested, and the framework does not answer it.</p>

  <h3 id="s4-5">4.5 Perspectival plurality</h3>
  <p>For beings in Tiers A–C, their way of experiencing the world may be difficult or impossible for us to reach. That a perspective is inaccessible is not evidence that it is absent (Fricker 2007). This is the content of the recognition condition in <a href="#s10">§10</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="s4-6">4.6 Representation</h3>
  <p>Some affected parties cannot take part directly: future generations, infants, animals, communities that lack institutional voice. Representation is legitimate only if (i) representatives are chosen through a process that can itself be contested, (ii) they state the basis on which they claim to know the interests concerned, (iii) where interests are contested there is more than one representative or perspective, (iv) they can be removed, (v) they are neither the actor nor dependent on the actor for appointment or funding, and (vi) they represent uncertainty rather than substituting invented certainty. Where the represented can express preferences, those take precedence over the representative's view of their best interests.</p>

  <p><strong>(vii) Collective contestation must not undermine individual contestation.</strong> Where a collective procedure is used to represent affected parties, the procedure must preserve the ability of individuals <em>within</em> the collective to contest the collective's decisions, and to seek independent review of them where the collective's own procedures fail. A collective that silences its members is not a substitute for individual standing. It is an additional layer of possible domination. The framework does not take a position on which traditions are legitimate. It requires that no individual's standing be extinguished by the fact that a collective has spoken.</p>

  <aside class="key-points">
    <ul>
      <li>Four tiers of standing: agents, welfare subjects, uncertain candidates, non-subjects.</li>
      <li>Tier C requires a <strong>plausibility threshold</strong>.</li>
      <li>Representation of silent parties is legitimate only under six conditions.</li>
      <li>Collective contestation must not undermine individual contestation.</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
</section>

<section id="s5">
  <h2><span class="num">5.</span>Interdependence, Difference and Non-Domination</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">Existence is relational. Symbiotic standing is the ideal that relational existence can fail to meet — and it must be measurable if the framework is to be taken seriously. §5.2 develops the framework's positive epistemic proposal.</p>

  <p>Living beings, institutions, technologies and ecosystems exist through relations of dependence and influence. Existence is relational: no being exists except through relations of dependence, influence and constitution — a child through care, an ecosystem through nutrient cycles, a person through language and recognition. This is not a metaphysical claim about the nature of being; it is an empirical and phenomenological one.</p>

  <p>Some of these relations are reciprocal and enabling, others unequal, extractive or harmful. The word "symbiosis" is used here with care. In biology it covers mutualism but also parasitism, so it cannot by itself carry a normative meaning. In this essay it names a normative ideal: relations of dependence in which the dependence is not exploited, difference is not forced into sameness, and the dependent party keeps the conditions for continued existence, development and participation (<a href="#s10">§10</a>).</p>

  <p>Answerability (<a href="#s2-2">§2.2</a>) is the procedure by which this ideal is pursued. Symbiotic standing is the horizon. Answerability is the road. But the relation between them is stronger than this image suggests: §5.2 argues that under conditions of asymmetric influence, answerability is not just the road to symbiotic standing, but the <em>heightened epistemic safeguard</em> for knowing whether symbiotic standing has been achieved.</p>

  <p>Harmony enforced by silencing, homogenising or sacrificing the vulnerable is not symbiotic. Difference is not a defect to be corrected. Different beings may perceive reality through entirely different capacities. The ability to explain something does not mean one has understood its full significance. This is why difference should be approached with curiosity rather than automatic correction. The asymmetry also runs both ways: the more capable may misunderstand the less capable, and the less capable cannot audit the more capable. That second half is why independent capacity for review (<a href="#s11">§11</a>) matters.</p>

  <h3 id="s5-1">5.1 Making symbiotic standing measurable (or admitting it is not)</h3>
  <p class="section-lede">If the framework states defeaters for its ground and failure conditions for its activation rule, its own ideal cannot be exempt from assessment.</p>

  <p>Symbiotic standing was defined above as a normative ideal: relations of dependence in which the dependence is not exploited, difference is not forced into sameness, and the dependent party keeps the conditions for continued existence, development and participation. If the framework's stated defeaters (<a href="#s2-5">§2.5</a>) and failure conditions (<a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a>) are taken seriously, symbiotic standing cannot remain a purely rhetorical horizon.</p>

  <p><strong>Three dimensions, specified conceptually apart from answerability. Their measurement may nevertheless depend on testimony, representation, and contestable evidence; conceptual distinction is not measurement independence.</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Non-exploitation (S1).</strong> The dependent party is not treated merely as an instrument for another party's benefit. This does not prohibit every unequal exchange, or every relationship in which one party benefits more than another. It requires that the benefits and burdens of the relationship be defensible in light of the interests and standing of those who depend on it. Measured by: the gap between stated preferences and accepted terms, adjusted for exit costs. A high gap indicates exploitation; a low gap indicates that the relation is not extractive, whatever else it is. The indicator is defined by the <em>dependent party's</em> stated preferences, not by the more powerful party's assessment of what those preferences should be. Stated preferences may be constrained, adaptive, strategic or difficult to elicit, so interpretation must remain open to challenge.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-assimilation (S2).</strong> Difference is not treated as a defect merely because it complicates the dominant actor's preferred arrangement. An intervention should distinguish between reducing an avoidable burden and eliminating a difference for the convenience of the more powerful party. A programme that seeks to make deaf children hearing is assimilation; a programme that provides sign language and accessible environments is not. The indicator is observable in the design of interventions, not in the intentions of actors. It also requires that the dependent party can identify which of their burdens are being addressed — an assessment the more powerful party cannot make on their behalf. The affected party's account is important evidence, not an infallible or exclusive verdict about the intervention.</li>
    <li><strong>Continued possibility (S3).</strong> The relationship preserves meaningful conditions for continued existence, development and participation. This dimension is temporal. A relationship cannot be judged only by whether it produces acceptable outcomes at a single moment. It must also be assessed by whether it preserves the conditions under which the dependent party can continue to act, develop, and revise its course. Measured by: whether F1–F5 (<a href="#s10">§10</a>) hold over time, not just at a single decision. A relation that satisfies F1–F5 at one moment and violates them at the next is not symbiotic; it is a relation that happened to be benign once.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What symbiosis does not mean</h4>
  <p>Symbiosis does not mean harmony, mutual benefit in every decision, or the absence of conflict. Nor does it mean that every party must receive everything it wants. It is a normative standard for evaluating relationships of dependence: whether the relationship avoids exploitation, forced assimilation, and the foreclosure of future possibilities. The three dimensions are not a complete theory of justice. They do not independently settle rights, resource allocation, or every conflict between legitimate interests. Their purpose is more limited: to identify conditions that a defensible relationship of dependence should protect.</p>

  <p><strong>What this does, and what it does not do.</strong> It does not make symbiotic standing a score. It makes it <em>contestable</em> in the same way the activation rule is: with anchors, counter-profiles, independent raters. If a relation scores low on S1–S3 across repeated assessments, the framework says it is not symbiotic — whatever the actors claim. If a relation scores high, the framework does not say it <em>is</em> symbiotic; it says the observable indicators are consistent with it.</p>

  <p><strong>Why this matters for the framework's integrity.</strong> The framework requires actors to answer for their influence. It would be incoherent for the framework's own ideal to be exempt from that requirement. A framework whose central ideal cannot be assessed is a framework with a hole in its centre. §5.1 closes the hole. §5.2 argues that the hole cannot be closed without answerability.</p>

  <aside class="key-points">
    <ul>
      <li>Existence is relational.</li>
      <li>Symbiotic standing is a normative ideal, specified through three observable dimensions: non-exploitation (S1), non-assimilation (S2), continued possibility (S3).</li>
      <li>Each dimension has a conceptual referent distinct from answerability, although assessing it may require contestable testimony and independent evidence.</li>
      <li>Each dimension requires the dependent party's own assessment — the more powerful party cannot specify what S1–S3 require on their behalf.</li>
      <li>The framework applies to its own ideal the standard it applies to actors.</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>

  <h3 id="s5-2">5.2 The epistemic burden principle (EBP)</h3>
  <p class="section-lede">Power does not make knowledge impossible. It changes what counts as adequate evidence.</p>

  <p>Asymmetric influence can create a particular epistemic problem: the actor who shapes another party's conditions may also shape the evidence used to assess those conditions. The actor may select the available indicators, determine which complaints are recorded, define what counts as success, and control the information available to reviewers. This does not mean that powerful actors are necessarily dishonest or incapable of understanding others. It means that their position can create systematic blind spots and conflicts of interest.</p>

  <aside class="key-prop" data-label="The epistemic burden principle (EBP)">
    <p>Where an actor exercises substantial discretionary influence over parties who lack effective means of correction, the actor bears a heightened burden to support claims about the effects and legitimacy of that influence with evidence not controlled solely by the actor, and with meaningful opportunities for affected parties or legitimate representatives to correct the account.</p>
    <p>This is a rebuttable burden of inquiry and justification. It is not a claim that answerability is the only route to knowledge, that affected parties are infallible, or that procedure is sufficient for justice.</p>
  </aside>

  <h4>5.2.1 Why the burden shifts</h4>
  <p>The argument has four steps.</p>
  <p><strong>First, the object of assessment is partly first-personal.</strong> Whether a relation is exploitative, assimilating or agency-preserving cannot always be determined from aggregate outcomes alone. A policy may improve a measured outcome while imposing a burden the metric omits; an intervention may preserve biological functioning while foreclosing meaningful choice. The experiences and judgments of affected parties are therefore relevant evidence, though not the only evidence.</p>
  <p><strong>Second, asymmetry affects the production and interpretation of evidence.</strong> The more powerful party may set the available options, define success, control records, determine which complaints are legible, and select the time horizon over which outcomes are measured. These mechanisms can produce systematic blind spots without requiring malice. The claim is probabilistic and institutional: asymmetry can shape what is seen, recorded and treated as credible. It does not establish that every powerful actor is biased or that every affected-party report is accurate.</p>
  <p><strong>Third, self-assessment is therefore insufficient as a default.</strong> When the actor controls both the influence and the evidential frame used to evaluate it, its conclusion is not independent confirmation. This does not make self-assessment worthless. It means that, as stakes and contestation deficits rise, the evidential burden should shift toward independent audit, transparent reasons, access to records, protected channels for dissent, and attention to the testimony of affected parties.</p>
  <p><strong>Fourth, the appropriate response is plural correction, not a single privileged voice.</strong> Affected parties should be able to correct descriptions of their own interests and experiences. Independent reviewers can test factual claims, examine aggregate effects and identify conflicts within groups. Representatives may be necessary where direct participation is impossible, but their authority must itself be contestable. No one source should be treated as infallible.</p>

  <h4>5.2.2 What follows — and what does not</h4>
  <p>The argument supports a <em>presumption against epistemic closure</em>: where power is asymmetric and affected parties cannot effectively contest, the powerful party must not treat its own view as conclusive. It does <em>not</em> prove that answerability is a logically necessary condition of knowledge. Knowledge may sometimes arise through reliable observation, independent science, trusted intermediaries, or other methods. Nor does it prove that participation by affected parties is sufficient: people may disagree, be misinformed, face adaptive preferences, or have interests that conflict.</p>
  <p>The framework's claim is instead comparative and normative. In conditions of asymmetric influence, a process that includes independent scrutiny and protected correction is generally better placed to detect errors than a process that leaves the actor as the sole author and judge of the evidence. This claim is defeasible. It should be weakened or rejected where such procedures systematically worsen outcomes, silence internal minorities, introduce new capture, or add burdens disproportionate to the risk.</p>

  <h4>5.2.3 Relation to existing traditions</h4>
  <p><em>Pettit.</em> Republican freedom as non-domination already makes contestability central. This framework does not claim that Pettit lacks a positive ideal or that it has discovered contestation. It attempts to connect non-domination to a graded account of answerability for discretionary influence, including cases where dependence is not reducible to state interference.</p>
  <p><em>Forst.</em> The right to justification already insists that those subject to norms must be able to demand and assess reasons. This framework does not supply the missing justification principle. It asks how the burden of producing evidence, preserving records and funding review should be allocated when the parties' capacities are unequal.</p>
  <p><em>Young.</em> The social connection model already explains responsibility for structural processes without reducing it to individual fault. ECP's proposed addition is a more explicit operational trigger and duty ladder. Whether that improves on Young's account is an open comparative question, not a settled gap in her theory.</p>
  <p><em>Epistemic injustice.</em> Fricker's work on testimonial and hermeneutical injustice already examines how credibility and interpretive power are distributed. The framework's contribution is not to have discovered that credibility is distributed unevenly, but to specify when the distribution of credibility becomes a structural feature of answerability itself.</p>
  <p><em>Care ethics.</em> Care theorists have developed sophisticated accounts of attentiveness, responsibility, competence and responsiveness. The framework's contribution is not to claim that care ethics has no procedure, but to insist that responsiveness must include channels through which the cared-for can challenge the carer's interpretation, alongside independent safeguards where direct voice is unavailable.</p>

  <h4>5.2.4 Defeaters and tests</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>B1 — No systematic epistemic risk.</strong> If evidence shows that asymmetry does not predictably affect what powerful actors notice, record or credit, the heightened-burden rationale is weakened.</li>
    <li><strong>B2 — Better alternatives.</strong> If another procedure provides equally reliable correction at lower cost and with less risk of capture or exclusion, it should replace the proposed safeguards.</li>
    <li><strong>B3 — Participation can worsen knowledge.</strong> If affected-party participation predictably entrenches misinformation, coercion, elite capture or internal silencing, the framework must specify countermeasures rather than romanticise participation.</li>
    <li><strong>B4 — Independent review is not independent.</strong> If reviewers reproduce the same interests, assumptions or exclusions as the actor, formal review does not discharge the burden.</li>
    <li><strong>B5 — Disproportionate burden.</strong> If the costs of the required process exceed its expected protective value in low-stakes cases, the activation thresholds and duty levels must be revised.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These defeaters do not make the claim immune to criticism. They identify the evidence and counterexamples that should change it. The proposal is not that every relation needs a tribunal, nor that every affected person must participate in every decision. The proposal is that the evidential burden should rise with the power to shape another's conditions and with the weakness of that person's means of correction.</p>

  <aside class="key-points">
    <ul>
      <li>Asymmetric influence creates a heightened risk of epistemic self-confirmation, not an impossibility of knowledge.</li>
      <li>The powerful actor should not close an inquiry using only evidence it controls.</li>
      <li>Affected-party testimony, independent review and transparent records are complementary sources of correction; none is infallible.</li>
      <li>The contribution is a graduated epistemic burden linked to discretion, severity, entrenchment and contestation deficit — not a claim that answerability is uniquely necessary for knowledge.</li>
      <li>Five defeaters (B1–B5) identify when the claim should be weakened, redesigned or abandoned.</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
</section>

<div class="part-divider" id="part-iii">
  <p class="part-divider__label">Part III</p>
  <p class="part-divider__title">When, and How Much</p>
</div>

<section id="s6">
  <h2><span class="num">6.</span>The Influence Profile</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">Five components, each assessed against observable indicators.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>Table 1. The five components of the influence profile</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Component</th>
          <th scope="col">Role</th>
          <th scope="col">Guiding question</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Discretion (D)</strong></td>
          <td>Gate</td>
          <td>Does the actor have genuine alternatives and a role in setting this influence?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Entrenchment (E)</strong></td>
          <td>Driver</td>
          <td>How long do the consequences last, and how hard are they to undo?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Severity (S)</strong></td>
          <td>Driver</td>
          <td>How much is at stake for the affected?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Contestation deficit (C)</strong></td>
          <td>Driver</td>
          <td>How hard is it for the affected to exit, object, or even find out what is done to them? This is where the operationalisation of AIP enters (§8.1).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Foreseeability (F)</strong></td>
          <td>Attribution</td>
          <td>What could the actor reasonably have known?</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Two clarifications. First, persistence refers to the consequences, not to the act. Second, foreseeability does not drive the level. It fixes what the actor must answer for, and generates a duty of inquiry proportional to the stakes.</p>

  <p>The five components do not discard a richer profile of influence; they reorganise it. <em>Magnitude</em> and <em>Scope</em> are subsumed within Severity. <em>Asymmetry</em> is subsumed within Contestation Deficit. <em>Control</em> is subsumed within Discretion. <em>Persistence</em> is subsumed within Entrenchment. Nothing is lost; the profile is made simpler by being made derivable.</p>

  <aside class="key-points">
    <ul>
      <li>Five components: <strong>Discretion</strong> (gate), <strong>Entrenchment</strong>, <strong>Severity</strong>, <strong>Contestation deficit</strong> (drivers), <strong>Foreseeability</strong> (attribution).</li>
      <li>Contestation deficit is where the operationalisation of AIP enters.</li>
      <li>Foreseeability does not drive the level; it fixes what the actor must answer for and generates a duty of inquiry.</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
</section>

<section id="s7">
  <h2><span class="num">7.</span>The Activation Rule</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">Discretion opens the gate. Entrenchment, severity and contestation deficit set the level — the <em>how much</em>. The duty ladder (§8) then specifies the <em>what kind</em>.</p>

  <h3 id="s7-1">7.1 The rule</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No discretion:</strong> the actor does not bear the duty for this influence. It shifts to whoever set the governing rule or could have. The actor still owes escalation (<a href="#s8-3">§8.3</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Discretion present:</strong> Level 1 always applies. It is the lightest duty and is owed even where exit and review work, because exit and review are only informed if the actor can say what it is doing.</li>
    <li><strong>Level 2</strong> applies if any one driver is High, or any two are Moderate.</li>
    <li><strong>Level 3</strong> applies if S is High and at least one of E or C is High.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Severity is judged first at the individual level. For decisions about the design of a system that acts on many people, it is also judged at the aggregate level (<a href="#s9-1">§9.1</a>). Where an actor lacks the evidence to exclude a credible severe outcome, it rates S on the credible worst case.</p>

  <h3 id="s7-2">7.2 Why these conditions</h3>
  <p>Each element can be derived from AIP (<a href="#s2-2">§2.2</a>).</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Discretion.</strong> Answerability is owed for what an agent chooses.</li>
    <li><strong>Entrenchment.</strong> What makes mistaken or unjustified influence tolerable is the possibility of correction afterwards. Entrenchment removes that channel.</li>
    <li><strong>Severity.</strong> The strength of a complaint scales with the burden it describes (<a href="#s12-2">§12.2</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Contestation deficit.</strong> Where the affected can exit, object effectively and understand what is done to them, they supply part of the accountability themselves (Hirschman 1970). The deficit measures how much must instead be supplied by the actor and by reviewers. It is the operational measure of how thoroughly exit and review fail.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="s7-3">7.3 What the rule is not</h3>
  <p>It is not a score and it applies no weights. It is not a verdict either. It yields a presumptive level that can be overridden only with stated reasons (<a href="#s9-3">§9.3</a>). It is a decision aid with its joints exposed. And it determines the <em>strength</em> of duty, not the <em>kind</em>. Which safeguard the duty addresses is determined separately, by the safeguard analysis in §8.</p>

  <aside class="key-points">
    <ul>
      <li>Discretion is the gate; E, S and C set the level: L1 always with discretion; L2 if any driver is High or two are Moderate; L3 if S is High and at least one of E or C is High.</li>
      <li>Each condition is derivable from AIP, not stipulated.</li>
      <li>The rule determines <em>how much</em> duty; §8 determines <em>which safeguard</em>.</li>
      <li>It is not a score and not a verdict.</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
</section>

<section id="s8">
  <h2><span class="num">8.</span>The Duty Ladder and Who Bears It</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">Three levels, each containing the one below. The activation rule (§7) sets <em>how much</em> duty; the safeguard analysis at each level sets <em>which safeguard</em> the duty addresses. The three safeguards are complementary, not interchangeable.</p>

  <h3 id="s8-1">8.1 The three safeguards are complementary, not interchangeable</h3>
  <p>AIP (<a href="#s2-2">§2.2</a>) does not require all three safeguards in every case. But it also does not allow one safeguard to replace another without examining what each is meant to protect. The three serve related but different functions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Exit</strong> protects the affected party's ability to withdraw from the relationship. It is only meaningful if informed, practically available, affordable, and free from disproportionate retaliation or transferred harm. A nominal right to leave is not meaningful where leaving means losing access to essential care, shelter, livelihood or participation in society.</li>
    <li><strong>Independent review</strong> protects the affected party against unilateral judgment by the actor. It requires sufficient independence, access to evidence, and authority to identify and address relevant failures. Formal review without these does not discharge the duty.</li>
    <li><strong>Contestable justification</strong> protects the affected party's ability to challenge the reasons and assumptions behind decisions. It cannot be reduced to the publication of an explanation that no one has the practical ability to question.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The activation rule (<a href="#s7">§7</a>) determines the level. The level determines what kind of safeguard analysis the actor owes.</p>

  <h3 id="s8-2">8.2 The levels</h3>
  <p><strong>Level 1: Explain.</strong> The actor can state, on request and in proportion to the matter, the purpose of the action, the interests considered, the alternatives considered and the known uncertainties. Level 1 is owed wherever discretion is present, because explanation is the precondition of any informed exit, review or contestation.</p>
  <p><strong>Level 2: Review and respond — with safeguard analysis.</strong> Level 1, plus: an accessible channel for objection; access to the relevant information; a reasoned response within a reasonable time; revision where warranted; a record of decisions and reasons; a reviewer other than the original decider where feasible; and evidence that review is not symbolic. In addition, Level 2 requires a <em>safeguard analysis</em>: which of exit, review or justification is most deficient in this relationship, and which remedies address that specific deficit? An actor who provides review but leaves exit nominal has not discharged Level 2 merely because review exists.</p>
  <p><strong>Level 3: Preserve, examine, authorise — with safeguard mix.</strong> Level 2, plus: preserved records and version history; independent examination meeting the conditions of <a href="#s11">§11</a>; a written assessment of less harmful alternatives; authorisation by a body outside the actor for severe or irreversible influence; staged implementation, reversibility by design or a sunset clause; remedy for those harmed; and post-implementation review. Level 3 also requires an explicit <em>safeguard mix</em>: which of exit, review and justification does this relationship need, what does each protect, and where is each weakest? Level 3 authorisation requires publication of the authorising body's R1 indicators before authorisation (<a href="#s11-4">§11.4</a>).</p>

  <h3 id="s8-3">8.3 Allocation by capacity</h3>
  <p>The level attaches to the influence, that is, to the arrangement. Duties are then allocated.</p>
  <p>Those with the capacity to establish mechanisms (institutions, system owners, funders, regulators) bear the duty to establish Level 2 and Level 3 mechanisms. Others along the chain owe their own Level 1 explanation, honest participation, escalation of concerns and non-obstruction. No scapegoating: a front-line actor is not answerable for structural deficits she cannot cure, though she remains answerable for her own discretion.</p>

  <h3 id="s8-4">8.4 Not authority</h3>
  <p>A higher level means stronger constraints on power, not greater authority. Level 3 does not make the most powerful actor the arbiter of its own case.</p>

  <aside class="key-points">
    <ul>
      <li>The three safeguards — exit, review, justification — are complementary, not interchangeable. Each protects something different.</li>
      <li>Level 1: explain (universal). Level 2: review and respond, with safeguard analysis. Level 3: preserve, examine, authorise, with explicit safeguard mix.</li>
      <li>Level 3 requires publication of the authorising body's R1 indicators before authorisation.</li>
      <li>The level attaches to the arrangement; duties are allocated by capacity. No scapegoating.</li>
      <li>Higher level means stronger constraints, not greater authority.</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
</section>

<section id="s9">
  <h2><span class="num">9.</span>Rating Protocol: Anchors, Hygiene and Sensitivity</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">Judgment cannot be eliminated. It can be made visible, checkable and contestable.</p>

  <h3 id="s9-1">9.1 Anchors</h3>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>Table 2. Anchors for the three drivers</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Driver</th>
          <th scope="col">Low</th>
          <th scope="col">Moderate</th>
          <th scope="col">High</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Entrenchment</strong></td>
          <td>Consequences brief, or easily undone at trivial cost; no lock-in</td>
          <td>Consequences last months to years, or undoing them takes substantial cost; some lock-in</td>
          <td>Effectively permanent or intergenerational; undoing is impossible or prohibitive</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Severity</strong></td>
          <td>Convenience, preference or easily substitutable goods</td>
          <td>Significant interests (income, opportunity, non-life-threatening health, important relationships), remediable</td>
          <td>Life, health, basic needs, basic agency, development</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Contestation deficit</strong></td>
          <td>Affected can exit at tolerable cost, know what is done, and have voice channels that lead to correction</td>
          <td>One of exit, information or effective voice meaningfully impaired</td>
          <td>Two or more impaired, or no exit and no effective voice</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Severity is rated on the most burdened individual first. Breadth does not raise individual severity. For decisions about system design, aggregate severity is rated on population-level effects. Capabilities-style accounts of what is at stake (Sen 2009; Nussbaum 2006) can be used to calibrate these ratings.</p>

  <h3 id="s9-2">9.2 Hygiene</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Rate before looking at the level the rule produces and before deciding what the actor wants to do.</li>
    <li>Use at least two raters, one independent of the actor where Level 2 or above is in question.</li>
    <li>Record disagreements instead of averaging them.</li>
    <li><strong>Sensitivity test.</strong> Move each rating one step up and down. If the level changes, the case is borderline.</li>
    <li><strong>Calibration.</strong> Compare the profile with the reference cases in <a href="#s14">§14</a>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3 id="s9-3">9.3 Reasoned override</h3>
  <p>The presumptive level may be overridden up or down only with written reasons. Overrides are recorded and, where safe, published.</p>

  <h3 id="s9-4">9.4 Counter-profiles</h3>
  <p>Any affected party or representative may file an alternative profile, and the actor must respond to the specific differences.</p>

  <aside class="key-points">
    <ul>
      <li>Anchors are observable indicators, not scores.</li>
      <li>Hygiene: rate before knowing the outcome; two raters; record disagreements; run the sensitivity test; calibrate against reference cases.</li>
      <li>Overrides require written reasons.</li>
      <li>Counter-profiles keep the framework's own categories contestable.</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
</section>

<div class="part-divider" id="part-iv">
  <p class="part-divider__label">Part IV</p>
  <p class="part-divider__title">Structure: Floor, Review, Power</p>
</div>

<section id="s10">
  <h2><span class="num">10.</span>The Floor: Minimum Conditions in Relationships of Dependence</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">Continued existence is not enough. A being can stay alive or operational while losing any say over its own circumstances.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>F1 — Exit or voice.</strong> Where possible, a meaningful opportunity to withdraw, refuse or communicate disagreement.</li>
    <li><strong>F2 — Contestation.</strong> Decisions are open to challenge regardless of the intelligence, authority or technical capability of the decider. Contestation may be collective, subject to §4.6 (vii).</li>
    <li><strong>F3 — Development.</strong> Opportunities for learning, adaptation and growth are preserved.</li>
    <li><strong>F4 — Non-foreclosure.</strong> The future possibilities of others are not unnecessarily closed.</li>
    <li><strong>F5 — Recognition.</strong> Interests and possible experiences are acknowledged rather than dismissed because they are hard to understand (Fricker 2007).</li>
  </ul>

  <p>F1 and F2 are the mirror image of the contestation deficit in <a href="#s7">§7</a>: where they fail, C rises.</p>

  <aside class="key-points">
    <ul>
      <li>Five minimum conditions (F1–F5): exit or voice, contestation, development, non-foreclosure, recognition.</li>
      <li>The Floor applies independently of the activation rule.</li>
      <li>Protection is not captivity.</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
</section>

<section id="s11">
  <h2><span class="num">11.</span>No Sole Judge: Review, Power and the Regress</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">No actor should be the sole judge of its own influence. The reviewer regress is a design problem, bounded by finality without infallibility — and by a precondition that converts diagnosis into design.</p>

  <h3 id="s11-1">11.1 The prohibition</h3>
  <blockquote>
    <p>An actor exercising substantial influence must remain answerable to forms of review that are meaningfully independent of its own interests and decisions.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>This does not assume that the powerful are malicious. Actors are prone to self-serving judgment and are limited in what they can see (<a href="#s2-4">§2.4</a>), and review that is not independent of the reviewed has little claim to legitimacy.</p>

  <h3 id="s11-2">11.2 The regress</h3>
  <p>If every reviewer needs a reviewer, accountability looks like an endless hierarchy. The response has three parts.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Finality without infallibility.</strong> Working systems end the regress with decisions that are final for practical purposes but can be reopened on new evidence, on new standing, on demonstrated procedural failure, or at a sunset.</li>
    <li><strong>Contestation is not adjudication.</strong> The reviewed and the affected may challenge a reviewer's independence or competence before a differently constituted body.</li>
    <li><strong>Plurality and publicity instead of a top.</strong> Several reviewers with different standpoints, publishing their reasons, make decentralised scrutiny possible (cf. Ostrom 1990).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="s11-3">11.3 Design conditions for review</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>R1</strong> Independent appointment, funding and removal.</li>
    <li><strong>R2</strong> Plurality.</li>
    <li><strong>R3</strong> Publicity.</li>
    <li><strong>R4</strong> Contestable reviewers.</li>
    <li><strong>R5</strong> Effectiveness.</li>
    <li><strong>R6</strong> Contestable rules.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Capture indicators:</strong> reviewers appointed or funded by the reviewed; a record of never dissenting; staff moving between reviewer and reviewed; reviewers denied access; chronic delay.</p>

  <h3 id="s11-4">11.4 Power over the framework itself: from diagnosis to design</h3>
  <p class="section-lede">The framework's earlier formulation treated the power regress as a limit it could not eliminate. That was too weak. It has an internal resource against capture, and the resource is T5.</p>

  <p>Who defined the categories? Who selected the reviewers? Who can dismiss them? Whose interests are excluded even where formally represented? These four questions are asked in every application (<a href="#s14-1">§14.1</a>, step 6). The deeper problem remains: powerful actors will shape how the framework is applied to them. No framework can eliminate this. But a framework can convert the problem from a concession into a design constraint.</p>

  <p><strong>The revised requirement.</strong> T5 must be a <em>precondition</em>, not a diagnosis. Level 3 authorisation (<a href="#s8-2">§8.2</a>) requires, as a condition of validity, that the authorising body publishes its R1 indicators <em>before</em> authorisation: who appointed its members, who funds it, how members can be removed, and the rate at which its findings have been reversed or ignored. An authorisation issued by a body that cannot or will not publish these indicators is not a Level 3 authorisation. It is a Level 2 authorisation with a Level 3 label.</p>

  <p><strong>Why this changes the framework's status.</strong> It does not eliminate the power regress — no framework can. But it converts the regress from a <em>concession</em> into a <em>design constraint</em>. An actor that wants Level 3 legitimacy must accept the conditions that make Level 3 legitimacy meaningful. If it refuses, the framework does not collapse; it <em>downgrades the actor's claim</em>.</p>

  <p><strong>What this does not do.</strong> It does not solve the problem of who appoints the appointer. A sufficiently powerful actor can capture the publication requirement itself. The framework cannot prevent this. What it can do is make the capture <em>visible</em>. Visibility is not prevention, but it is the precondition of contestation. This is the sense in which the framework <em>handles</em> domination rather than <em>solves</em> it — and this is the point at which the power objection (<a href="#s2-6">§2.6</a>) presses hardest.</p>

  <aside class="key-points">
    <ul>
      <li>No actor should be the sole judge of its own influence.</li>
      <li>The regress is treated as a design problem: <strong>finality without infallibility</strong>, contestation not adjudication, plurality and publicity instead of a top.</li>
      <li>Review must meet six conditions (R1–R6). Capture indicators are named.</li>
      <li>T5 is a precondition of Level 3 authorisation.</li>
      <li>The framework handles domination; it does not solve it. The power objection (<a href="#s2-6">§2.6</a>) presses hardest at exactly this point.</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
</section>

<section id="s12">
  <h2><span class="num">12.</span>Conflicts, Ecology and Future Generations</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">A framework of answerability must confront a difficult fact: affected parties can have legitimate but incompatible interests. Procedure cannot settle every conflict — but it can discipline how conflicts are addressed.</p>

  <h3 id="s12-1">12.1 Why procedure cannot settle every conflict</h3>
  <p>A policy may protect one group while imposing unavoidable burdens on another. Ecological protection may restrict human activities. A medical decision may distribute scarce resources between people with equally serious needs. No procedure can make every conflict disappear. A justification process can reveal reasons, correct factual errors, and expose arbitrary decisions. It cannot guarantee that all legitimate claims can be satisfied simultaneously.</p>
  <p>The framework must therefore distinguish between:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Standing:</strong> whose interests and claims deserve consideration?</li>
    <li><strong>Constraints:</strong> which actions are impermissible regardless of aggregate benefit?</li>
    <li><strong>Priority:</strong> how should unavoidable burdens be distributed?</li>
    <li><strong>Residual loss:</strong> what must be acknowledged when no available option avoids serious harm?</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="s12-2">12.2 A priority structure for conflicting claims</h3>
  <p>The following structure is proposed as a disciplined method, not a universal algorithm.</p>

  <h4 id="s12-2-1">12.2.1 First: protect basic standing</h4>
  <p>No party should be excluded from consideration merely because it has less intelligence, influence, productivity, social status, or ability to articulate its interests. The ability to speak is relevant to participation, but it is not the sole basis of moral standing. Where direct participation is impossible, representation must be cautious, plural and open to challenge (<a href="#s4-6">§4.6</a>).</p>

  <h4 id="s12-2-2">12.2.2 Second: respect substantive constraints</h4>
  <p>Procedural legitimacy does not automatically make an outcome permissible. Basic rights, protections against severe abuse, and relevant legal or moral constraints remain substantive limits on what may be justified. A process cannot legitimise every outcome simply because it was transparent or participatory.</p>

  <h4 id="s12-2-3">12.2.3 Third: compare burdens individually</h4>
  <p>Where several alternatives remain permissible, decision-makers should examine the burdens each alternative imposes on each affected party. The question should not begin with aggregate benefit alone. It should ask whether one option imposes a particularly severe burden on a party when another option could avoid or reduce that burden without creating an equally serious one elsewhere. This is a form of individual comparison rather than a simple calculation of total welfare (Scanlon 1998).</p>

  <h4 id="s12-2-4">12.2.4 Fourth: distribute unavoidable burdens fairly</h4>
  <p>Where serious burdens cannot be avoided, their distribution should consider: the severity and duration of the burden; whether the affected party is already disadvantaged; whether the party contributed to the risk; whether the party has realistic alternatives; whether the burden is reversible; and whether those imposing the burden are also required to bear a proportionate share of its costs. Aggregation may sometimes be necessary, but it should not automatically override the most serious individual claims.</p>

  <h4 id="s12-2-5">12.2.5 Fifth: preserve future possibilities</h4>
  <p>Where alternatives are otherwise comparable, preference should be given to options that preserve a wider range of future possibilities, especially when the alternative is irreversible. This is not an absolute prohibition on irreversible decisions. Some emergencies and necessary interventions cannot avoid them. It is a reason to demand stronger justification as the scale and irreversibility of an intervention increase (cf. Jonas 1984).</p>

  <h4 id="s12-2-6">12.2.6 Sixth: acknowledge residual loss</h4>
  <p>When no option can satisfy all legitimate claims, the decision-maker must not describe the result as though no injustice, sacrifice or loss occurred. A defensible decision should identify: whose interests were not met; why the competing claim prevailed; what alternatives were considered; what mitigation remains possible; what compensation or restoration is appropriate; and what should be reconsidered if circumstances change. A decision can be justified without being harmless. This distinction is essential. Otherwise, the language of justification risks turning unavoidable loss into an appearance of moral success.</p>

  <h3 id="s12-3">12.3 Emergencies</h3>
  <p>An immediate and serious threat may justify acting before the full answerability procedure can be completed. But emergency authority must remain limited. It requires: necessity; proportionality; a restricted scope; contemporaneous recording of reasons where possible; an automatic time limit or sunset; subsequent independent review; and correction or remedy where the action caused avoidable harm.</p>
  <p>An emergency may justify postponing ordinary procedure. It does not create permanent exemption from answerability.</p>

  <h3 id="s12-4">12.4 Ecological integrity and future generations</h3>
  <p>Ecological systems cannot be represented adequately by the preferences of individual human participants alone. Their protection may be justified through the interests of present and future beings, the conditions on which life depends, and the values people hold regarding the natural world. The framework does not claim that ecosystems must be assigned precisely the same form of standing as persons. It does claim that uncertainty about the full significance of ecological systems should not be treated as proof that their destruction is morally insignificant (<a href="#s4-1">§4.1</a>).</p>
  <p>For future generations, the duty is primarily condition-regarding: preserve the conditions under which future people can meet basic needs and exercise meaningful agency. It is not a duty to determine their future choices. Because it concerns conditions and not particular individuals, it does not depend on who will exist (cf. the non-identity problem, Parfit 1984). Where actions are irreversible and the potential consequences are severe, the burden of justification should increase.</p>

  <h3 id="s12-5">12.5 No automatic hierarchy</h3>
  <p>Intelligence, productivity, population size and technical sophistication do not by themselves determine moral priority. Weight follows the nature and strength of the interests at stake, not a being's rank on an imagined scale of worth.</p>

  <aside class="key-points">
    <ul>
      <li>Procedure cannot settle every conflict. The framework distinguishes standing, constraints, priority and residual loss.</li>
      <li>Six-step priority structure: protect basic standing; respect substantive constraints; compare burdens individually; distribute unavoidable burdens fairly; preserve future possibilities; acknowledge residual loss.</li>
      <li>Emergencies create no standing exemptions: sunset, record, review.</li>
      <li>Ecological integrity is protected through dependent beings, future generations and held values.</li>
      <li>Future generations: preserve conditions and options, not particular choices.</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
</section>
      <section id="s13">
        <h2><span class="num">13.</span>Learning, Correction and Reversibility</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">No actor, institution or intelligence should assume its understanding is complete.</p>

        <p><strong>The duty to learn.</strong> Where capacity exists and consequences are significant, actors monitor outcomes, investigate unexpected results, listen to criticism and update.</p>
        <p><strong>Reversibility by design.</strong> Consequential decisions should be built to be revisable: staged implementation, monitoring, independent evaluation, defined conditions for reconsideration, and sunset clauses.</p>
        <p><strong>Self-application.</strong> The framework is itself an influence on those who adopt it and on those affected by their adoption. It is subject to its own principles. Versions are dated and public, criticism is invited, counter-profiles apply to its own application, and <a href="#s15">§15</a> states how it can be tested and replaced.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>The duty to learn is a duty where capacity exists and consequences are significant.</li>
            <li>Reversibility by design reduces the risk that incomplete understanding becomes permanent.</li>
            <li>The framework is subject to its own principles.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <div class="part-divider" id="part-v">
        <p class="part-divider__label">Part V</p>
        <p class="part-divider__title">Application and Testing</p>
      </div>

      <section id="s14">
        <h2><span class="num">14.</span>Application: A Nine-Step Procedure and Worked Cases</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">The same procedure yields very different obligations for different cases. A framework that gave every case the same answer would be useless.</p>

        <h3 id="s14-1">14.1 The procedure</h3>
        <ol>
          <li>Identify the relationship. Who is affected, in which tier (<a href="#s4">§4</a>), and what dependencies exist?</li>
          <li>Identify actors and discretion.</li>
          <li>Rate E, S and C against the anchors (<a href="#s9-1">§9.1</a>), and F for attribution. Use two raters and record reasons.</li>
          <li>Apply the activation rule (<a href="#s7">§7</a>) and run the sensitivity test.</li>
          <li>Check the Floor (F1–F5, <a href="#s10">§10</a>).</li>
          <li>Analyse power (<a href="#s11-4">§11.4</a>).</li>
          <li>Examine alternatives and unrepresented interests (<a href="#s12-2">§12.2</a>).</li>
          <li>Allocate duties by capacity (<a href="#s8-3">§8.3</a>) and set up the safeguard analysis/mix (<a href="#s8-1">§8.1</a>–<a href="#s8-2">§8.2</a>) and review that meets R1–R6.</li>
          <li>Reassess on stated triggers, and invite counter-profiles.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 id="s14-2">14.2 Case: AI triage in intensive care</h3>
        <p>A hospital deploys an AI system to prioritise ICU admissions during a respiratory pandemic. Entrenchment is High, severity is High, and contestation deficit is High. Level 3.</p>
        <p>What Level 3 requires: preserved model versions, descriptions of training data, individual scores and override records; an independent ethics board with lay and patient representation; a written comparison with clinician-only triage; authorisation from the public-health authority; a sunset clause; and a remedy for those wrongly denied.</p>
        <p><strong>Safeguard mix (Level 3):</strong> exit is impossible in a pandemic, so justification and independent review must carry the load. The safeguard analysis shows that review is the weakest element unless the ethics committee is made genuinely independent of the hospital board.</p>
        <p><strong>Power analysis:</strong> the categories were set by the vendor and clinical leadership, and the ethics committee is appointed by the hospital board, which fails R1 unless changed. A hybrid in which the AI recommends and a human decides, with the decision documented, is the default preference because it keeps contestation alive.</p>
        <p>The framework does not decide the case. It tells the hospital what it owes an answer to, and to whom.</p>

        <h3 id="s14-2b">14.2b Case: the emergency physician (a counterintuitive result)</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">Every other case in this essay produces the answer a careful reader would predict. This one does not.</p>

        <p>A physician in an emergency department must decide, in under ninety seconds, whether to intubate a patient. The activation rule gives Level 3.</p>

        <p><strong>The counterintuitive result.</strong> Level 3 requires independent authorisation, preserved records and a written assessment of alternatives — <em>before the act</em>. A reader's first reaction is that this is absurd: the physician cannot convene an ethics board in ninety seconds.</p>

        <p><strong>The reply.</strong> The Level 3 duty does not fall on the physician in the moment. It falls on the <em>arrangement</em> (<a href="#s8-3">§8.3</a>): on the hospital, the protocols, the audit trail, the retrospective review, and the training. The physician's own duty is Level 1 in the moment and Level 2 afterwards. The ninety-second decision is <em>answerable</em>, not <em>authorised in advance</em>. It is authorised by the structure that made it possible, and that structure is the duty-bearer of Level 3.</p>

        <p><strong>Why this case is included.</strong> It looks like a <em>reductio</em> of the framework. The reply is that the reader has misidentified the duty-bearer, not that the framework is wrong. A framework that survives its own apparent <em>reductio</em> is stronger than one that only survives confirmation. But the reply can be challenged: if the framework relocates every hard demand to "the arrangement", it may become untestable. That is a real worry, and it is one of the things the tests in <a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a> are meant to probe.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>The emergency physician case appears to refute the framework.</li>
            <li>The reply: the Level 3 duty falls on the arrangement, not on the physician in the moment.</li>
            <li>A framework that survives its own apparent <em>reductio</em> is stronger than one that only survives confirmation — but the reply can be challenged, and the tests are meant to probe whether it is a genuine relocation or an escape.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <h3 id="s14-3">14.3 Case: a platform's recommendation system</h3>
        <p>For individual recommendations: Level 2. For the design of the system: Level 3, with preserved version history, independent audit, published aggregate metrics, and a sunset for any optimisation that cannot show population-level reversibility. "Engagement" is defined by the platform, audits are typically controlled by it, and non-users affected through the public sphere are excluded.</p>
        <p><strong>Safeguard analysis:</strong> at Level 2 (individual recommendations), the platform's users typically have nominal exit (leave the platform) but this is not meaningful given network effects; review exists but is controlled by the platform; justification is published but not contestable. Safeguard analysis at Level 2 requires that the platform identify which safeguard is most deficient — here, independent review — and address it before claiming Level 2 discharge.</p>

        <h3 id="s14-4">14.4 Case: a community health worker</h3>
        <p>She has little institutional power. The rule says the arrangement requires Level 3. But she has no capacity to build independent review. Under <a href="#s8-3">§8.3</a>, the duty to establish mechanisms falls on the programme, the ministry and the funders. Her personal duties are to explain her decisions, respond to objections within her role, and escalate when protocols fail. The level belongs to the arrangement; the duties are allocated by capacity.</p>

        <h3 id="s14-5">14.5 Four short cases</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>One-off organism release.</strong> Level 3, although the act happens once.</li>
          <li><strong>Dating platform age penalty.</strong> Level 2.</li>
          <li><strong>Café menu change.</strong> Level 1 only, and arguably not even that.</li>
          <li><strong>Physician following a protocol.</strong> No discretion over the protocol's content; the duty to justify the content moves to its designers.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 id="s14-5b">14.5b Case: emergency water rationing (duties conflict)</h3>
        <p>A water authority must impose rationing within 48 hours in a severe drought. Entrenchment and severity are High (crop loss and harm to people with medical dependence are not undone) and contestation deficit is High, so the rule gives Level 3, whose prior independent authorisation takes weeks. <a href="#s12-3">§12.3</a> allows acting first. The duties then split.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Not compressible:</strong> published reasons, preserved records, a contestation channel open from day one, exemptions for life-critical dependence, and a sunset clause.</li>
          <li><strong>Compressible:</strong> prior authorisation, replaced by review completed before the sunset.</li>
          <li><strong>Not decided by the framework:</strong> which rationing rule is fair, and whether 48 hours really precludes prior review. Raters may disagree; under <a href="#s9-2">§9.2</a> the disagreement is recorded, not averaged away.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Residual loss:</strong> whichever rule is chosen, some legitimate claims will not be met. Under <a href="#s12-2-6">§12.2.6</a>, the authority must identify whose interests were not met, why the competing claim prevailed, and what mitigation or restoration remains possible.</p>
        <p><strong>Failure mode:</strong> if "emergency" is routinely invoked to skip prior review, test T5 (<a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a>) is triggered. Here the framework imposes a cost on the actor's preferred option and admits residual dispute.</p>

