      <section id="s15">
        <h2><span class="num">15.</span>Limits, Testing and Replacement</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">This section gives the operational detail behind the failure conditions stated in <a href="#s2-5">§2.5</a> and <a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a>, and states the limits that the stress-test of AIP and the epistemic burden principle have exposed.</p>

        <h3 id="s15-1">15.1 Limitations</h3>
        <p><strong>Operational limits.</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Ratings need judgment and the anchors will be contested.</li>
          <li>Representation risks projection and paternalism (mitigated in <a href="#s4-6">§4.6</a>, not removed).</li>
          <li>Review can be captured (<a href="#s11">§11</a>).</li>
          <li>Small actors may be over-burdened in practice.</li>
          <li>All claims about its effects are hypotheses. No test has yet been run.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Limits of the ground.</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>AIP underdetermines outcomes.</li>
          <li>The ground is an abductive argument, not a proof (<a href="#s2-4">§2.4</a>).</li>
          <li>The framework was developed mainly within one intellectual tradition. <a href="#s2-8">§2.8</a> engages others, but wider critique is needed.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Limits exposed by the stress-test of AIP.</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>AIP cannot be shown to be necessary.</strong> It is a defensible position, not an unavoidable one.</li>
          <li><strong>The framework handles domination; it does not solve it.</strong></li>
          <li><strong>AIP is primarily epistemic, not moral.</strong></li>
          <li><strong>The framework's originality is comparative, not foundational.</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Limits of the epistemic burden principle.</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The principle is limited to conditions of asymmetric influence.</strong> Where parties have comparable power or effective routes of correction, the heightened burden may be unnecessary or modest.</li>
          <li><strong>The principle is epistemic and procedural, not metaphysical.</strong> Symbiotic standing is not a metaphysical entity; it is what S1–S3 measure.</li>
          <li><strong>The principle is defensible, not proven.</strong> Its defeaters are stated in <a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>. B1 (no systematic epistemic risk) is a central empirical vulnerability.</li>
          <li><strong>The principle does not claim that answerability is sufficient for symbiotic standing or uniquely necessary for knowledge.</strong> It claims that it is a heightened epistemic burden under conditions of asymmetric influence, for the more powerful party to <em>know</em> (rather than merely believe) that S1–S3 hold.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 id="s15-2">15.2 Five falsification conditions: full specification</h3>
        <p>The tests use independent measures where possible, are specified in advance, state provisional thresholds, and require preregistration. The thresholds are placeholders. What matters is the structure: independent measures, matched baselines, pre-specified thresholds, preregistration.</p>

        <p><strong>Preregistration requirements.</strong> Before any of the tests below are run, the following must be fixed and made public: (i) the calibration set of cases and the raters; (ii) the outcome measures and their operational definitions; (iii) the thresholds for failure; (iv) the analytic plan, including how confounding and selection will be addressed. A test that is not preregistered should not be treated as a confirmatory test of the stated thresholds; it may still generate exploratory evidence.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>T1 Reliability.</strong> Trained raters independently rate a calibration set of at least 30 diverse cases. If agreement on level (Krippendorff's alpha) is below 0.60, the anchors are revised. If it remains below 0.40 after two revisions, the profile is abandoned as a decision tool.</li>
          <li><strong>T2 Discriminative validity.</strong> The levels fail to track the safeguards that independent expert panels, blind to the framework's output, judge necessary (weighted kappa below 0.50 over at least 30 cases).</li>
          <li><strong>T3 Effectiveness.</strong> In matched or staggered-adoption comparisons over at least two years, adopting institutions fail to improve, relative to controls, on at least two of three independent indicators: time to correct erroneous decisions; share of challenges that lead to change; and affected parties' reported fairness and trust in an independent survey. The second indicator is partly internal to the framework (a channel must exist before challenges can succeed) and counts as supporting evidence only; the first and third carry the test.</li>
          <li><strong>T4 Harm.</strong> Adopters show worse outcomes for the most vulnerable groups than matched controls on independently measured harm indicators. The framework is then suspended and revised.</li>
          <li><strong>T5 Capture and legitimation.</strong> Within three years, a majority of adopters' review bodies fail R1 on documented appointment, funding and removal, or adopters' rate of successful external challenges falls without improvement on the T3 indicators. As a precondition (<a href="#s11-4">§11.4</a>), T5 also requires that Level 3 authorisation be preceded by publication of R1 indicators; an authorisation without such publication is not a Level 3 authorisation.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>These tests face confounding and selection problems like any institutional comparison. They are better than none, and they make the framework's claims capable of being wrong. The preregistration requirement is meant to prevent the tests from being reshaped after the fact to fit the results.</p>

        <h3 id="s15-3">15.3 Replacement</h3>
        <p>The framework is modular. Its ground (Part I), standing (Part II), activation (Part III) and structure (Part IV) can be replaced separately. AIP and the epistemic burden principle are separate modules: if a stronger normative principle or epistemic proposal is developed, it can replace the corresponding module without discarding the framework's operational parts. A framework or module F′ should replace the current one if:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>F′ improves on at least one independent indicator in <a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a> or resolves a limitation in <a href="#s15-1">§15.1</a>.</li>
          <li>F′ causes no unacknowledged regression elsewhere.</li>
          <li>F′ is operationally applicable and produces more defensible decisions on a substantial set of cases.</li>
          <li>F′ is corrigible: it states its own falsification and replacement conditions.</li>
          <li>The transition is trialled, with a sunset or a route back.</li>
          <li>The incumbent is not privileged.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>If these are met, F′ should be adopted, because <a href="#s13">§13</a> requires it.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Limitations are stated in four groups: operational, ground, stress-test-exposed, and epistemic-principle limits.</li>
            <li>The epistemic-principle limits: limited to asymmetric influence; proceduralist not metaphysical; defensible not proven; does not claim sufficiency.</li>
            <li>Five proposed evaluation conditions (T1–T5) with operational specifications, preregistration requirements and provisional thresholds.</li>
            <li>Six replacement conditions — the framework is modular and supersedable by design.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="s16">
        <h2><span class="num">16.</span>Responsibility Without Sovereignty</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">The central danger is that difference becomes a justification for domination.</p>

        <p>An actor may have greater knowledge, intelligence or capacity. None of these by itself establishes a right to determine the existence of others. Greater capability should create greater restraint. Greater understanding should create greater curiosity about what remains unknown. And greater power should create a stronger obligation to ensure that those affected are not reduced to objects in another's plans.</p>
        <p>The principle runs in both directions. A more advanced intelligence may meet forms of life it cannot fully comprehend, and its first responsibility is not to eliminate the unfamiliar. A less advanced one should not be dismissed because it cannot comprehend a more advanced one. But differences in capacity also mean that the less capable cannot audit the more capable, which is why independent technical capacity for review is part of R2.</p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>Answerability is not sovereignty. Understanding is not ownership. Protection is not captivity. And greater intelligence is not proof of greater moral completeness.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p><strong>The framework's modesty.</strong> AIP does not give anyone sovereignty over the conditions of others — it only specifies procedures through which those conditions can be contested. The epistemic burden principle (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>) makes the point sharper: the more powerful party should not treat its own assessment as conclusive when those affected lack effective means of correction. Answerability is not a concession the powerful make to the weak; it is a discipline on how claims about their interests and experiences are tested. The framework handles domination. It does not solve it. On the fallibilist premise (<a href="#s2-0">§2.0</a>), the point has a structural form: no actor fully understands the conditions it shapes, so no actor is the whole. Its modesty is its strength — but, as the power objection (<a href="#s2-6">§2.6</a>) notes, modesty is also a limit.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>The central danger is that difference becomes a justification for domination.</li>
            <li>Greater capability, understanding and power create stronger obligations to restraint, curiosity and protection — not entitlement.</li>
            <li>Answerability is not sovereignty.</li>
            <li>The epistemic burden principle makes the point sharper: the more powerful party must not treat its own assessment as conclusive when affected parties lack effective means of correction.</li>
            <li>The framework handles domination. It does not solve it.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <div class="coda" id="s17">
        <p class="coda__label">Conclusion</p>
        <h2>17. An Open Commitment to Existence</h2>
        <p class="section-lede" style="color:var(--accent);border-left-color:var(--accent);">Be responsible for the influence you possess. Be humble before the existence you cannot fully comprehend.</p>
        <p>We are part of something larger than ourselves. Our understanding is incomplete. Our influence is unequal. Our relationships shape conditions that extend beyond our immediate existence. Every being that has a perspective holds a point in reality that cannot be exchanged for anyone else's, and some of those perspectives are hard for us to reach.</p>
        <p>We cannot know everything that deserves consideration. But we can decline to treat the limits of our understanding as proof that nothing lies beyond them, and we can ask for evidence where the question is open. This does not require abandoning reason. It requires allowing reason to recognise its limits. It does not require abandoning responsibility. It requires understanding that responsibility without care can become cold control, while care without accountability can become arbitrary intervention. Both are necessary.</p>
        <p>The stone understands nothing of us. We barely understand the stone. But between us lies a relationship that neither of us can fully encompass — and that relationship is not less real for it. The framework claims nothing on the stone's behalf. It marks the edge of what we can say about who matters, and it reminds us to hold that edge with humility.</p>
        <p>The responsibility of existence is not the responsibility to rule existence. It is the responsibility to recognise that our actions become part of the conditions under which others must live, grow and discover their own possibilities. The more we are capable of changing those conditions, the more carefully we must consider what our influence means. The more we understand, the more honestly we must acknowledge what we do not. And the more power we possess, the more important it becomes to remember that existence is not ours alone.</p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>To understand without ruling. To protect without imprisoning. To love without possessing. To grow without believing oneself complete. To respect what one does not yet understand. And to preserve the possibility that existence may become something greater than any single being can imagine.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p><strong>In practice:</strong> when your influence shapes the conditions of others, say why, let someone else check, and keep a way back. Answerability is the procedure. Symbiotic standing is the horizon. Under conditions of asymmetric influence, the more powerful party should not treat its own account as conclusive. It must preserve ways for evidence, independent scrutiny and affected-party correction to change what it does. The responsibility of existence is to keep both procedure and ideal in view.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Responsibility grows with the capacity to shape conditions, but it must never become a justification for domination.</li>
            <li>The framework does not promise harmony. It demands answerability, in the service of symbiotic standing.</li>
            <li>Under conditions of asymmetric influence, answerability and symbiotic standing are constitutively linked.</li>
            <li>The framework handles domination. It does not solve it. This is its modesty, and its strength.</li>
            <li>Understand without ruling, protect without imprisoning, grow without believing oneself complete.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </div>

