      <section id="objections">
        <h2>Common objections</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Twelve objections the framework has to survive. Each is a real objection; none is fatal — but the first is the hardest, and the second and eleventh are the ones the framework most needs to answer.</p>

        <div class="objection">
          <h3><span class="obj-num">1.</span>"Why must the powerful justify themselves to the affected? What if they simply refuse?"</h3>
          <p class="reply">This is the hardest objection, and the framework does not pretend to refute it. The reply has four parts. First, AIP cannot be shown to be necessary. A determined realist cannot be argued out of his position, because argument is the practice he rejects. This is a limit stated in <a href="#s15-1">§15.1</a>. Second, the realist who <em>argues</em> against AIP is already engaged in the practice AIP describes. This is a performative presupposition, not a refutation. Third, the framework makes realist capture visible and costly. Fourth, and most importantly, the epistemic burden principle (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>) makes the point sharper: the powerful party cannot <em>know</em> whether the relation is symbiotic without the affected party's contestation. The refusal to justify is not just a moral failing; it is an epistemic one. The realist who refuses answerability does not gain knowledge of the relation. He loses it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="objection">
          <h3><span class="obj-num">2.</span>"This is a values framework dressed up as a procedure."</h3>
          <p class="reply">Partly, and the essay says so (<a href="#s2-9">§2.9</a>). AIP carries a minimal commitment to the equal standing of those affected. The claim is breadth of acceptability, not neutrality. A fair test is whether people with different comprehensive views can use it to reach different answers in the same case. They can: it does not decide the ICU case.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="objection">
          <h3><span class="obj-num">3.</span>"Proceduralism is itself a form of power. It gives the powerful a new vocabulary for doing what they were going to do anyway."</h3>
          <p class="reply">This is the second-hardest objection, and it is treated at length in <a href="#s2-6">§2.6</a>. The framework does not deny it. Its reply has three parts: (i) the objection is partly correct, and the framework's T5-as-precondition is its internal response; (ii) the objection is not specific to proceduralism — any normative framework can be captured; (iii) the framework's modesty is not an admission of failure but a claim about what any framework can do. A sufficiently powerful actor can capture the publication requirement itself. The framework can make the capture visible, but visibility is not prevention.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="objection">
          <h3><span class="obj-num">4.</span>"Why should contestation override better outcomes?"</h3>
          <p class="reply">It does not always. Where exit and review are available and effective, justification need not be owed (<a href="#s2-2">§2.2</a>). AIP is proportionate, not absolute. It privileges justification where exit and review fail. The remaining disagreement is genuine, and the framework sides with contestation in severe cases for epistemic reasons, while permitting temporary override in emergencies subject to sunset and review (<a href="#s12-3">§12.3</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="objection">
          <h3><span class="obj-num">5.</span>"The capacity principle legitimises power."</h3>
          <p class="reply">Answerability is not authority (<a href="#s3-2">§3.2</a>, <a href="#s8-4">§8.4</a>). Higher levels mean stronger constraints. T5 looks for exactly this misuse. The risk remains and is not argued away; <a href="#s11-4">§11.4</a> converts it from a concession into a design constraint. The framework handles this risk; it does not eliminate it. See also objection 3 and <a href="#s2-6">§2.6</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="objection">
          <h3><span class="obj-num">6.</span>"Who reviews the reviewers?"</h3>
          <p class="reply">The regress is bounded, not eliminated (<a href="#s11">§11</a>): finality without infallibility, review conditions R1–R6, plural and public reasoning, and counter-profiles. It also needs a political precondition, a balance of power, that no framework can supply by itself.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="objection">
          <h3><span class="obj-num">7.</span>"Precaution under uncertainty is either paralysing or too weak to matter."</h3>
          <p class="reply">The credibility threshold in <a href="#s4-2">§4.2</a> answers the first: reasons arise only where there is evidence or a plausible mechanism. Against the second: in Tier C the principle does real work, requiring investigation and proportionate safeguards. The stone does not meet the threshold and is not claimed to.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="objection">
          <h3><span class="obj-num">8.</span>"The ratings are arbitrary or manipulable."</h3>
          <p class="reply">Judgment cannot be eliminated, but <a href="#s9">§9</a> exposes it: anchors with observable indicators, two raters, a sensitivity test, reasoned overrides and counter-profiles. Tests T1 and T2 examine consistency, and <a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a> requires preregistration.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="objection">
          <h3><span class="obj-num">9.</span>"This is Western liberal philosophy in disguise."</h3>
          <p class="reply">Its development lies mostly in that tradition, and <a href="#s15-1">§15.1</a> says so. It does not claim universality for AIP. It claims that four considerations point toward it (<a href="#s2-1">§2.1</a>) and engages other traditions (<a href="#s2-8">§2.8</a>). That engagement shaped the text: contestation and representation may be collective, ecosystem standing is left open, and the boundary of who counts is not treated as settled.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="objection">
          <h3><span class="obj-num">10.</span>"It will burden the already overburdened."</h3>
          <p class="reply">The level attaches to the arrangement and duties are allocated by capacity (<a href="#s8-3">§8.3</a>). The health worker case (<a href="#s14-4">§14.4</a>) and the emergency physician case (<a href="#s14-2b">§14.2b</a>) show the mechanism. Whether burdens migrate to front-line staff is part of what tests T3 and T4 would reveal.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="objection">
          <h3><span class="obj-num">11.</span>"The epistemic burden principle is circular. It smuggles answerability into the definition of symbiotic standing."</h3>
          <p class="reply">This is the most serious objection to the framework's positive contribution, and the reply is in <a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>. S1–S3 are specified independently of answerability. S1 measures the gap between stated preferences and accepted terms, adjusted for exit costs. S2 measures whether interventions aim at removing the burden or the difference. S3 measures whether F1–F5 hold over time. These are observable without invoking any procedure of answerability. The principle adds a substantive claim: these three conditions can only be <em>reliably assessed</em> — and therefore only <em>reliably secured</em> — through procedures of answerability. This is not tautological. It is a claim about the epistemic conditions under which S1–S3 can be known. The principle may be wrong — the argument can be challenged, and the defeaters are stated in <a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a> — but the revised formulation does not define symbiotic standing in terms of answerability. If the reader finds the reply unconvincing, that is itself a useful test of the framework.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="objection">
          <h3><span class="obj-num">12.</span>"Why not just rely on rights and regulation?"</h3>
          <p class="reply">Rights and regulation are necessary and are not replaced. They leave gaps: influence by actors the law does not reach, structural harms not yet recognised as rights violations, and cases where the law lags. The framework helps decide what to demand of actors where law is silent.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

