      <section id="glossary">
        <h2>Glossary</h2>
        <dl class="glossary">
          <dt>Answerable Influence Principle (AIP)</dt>
          <dd>The framework's central normative principle: an actor that exercises consequential discretionary influence over conditions on which another party significantly depends, and that party lacks effective means to prevent, exit or correct the influence, owes proportionate answerability to those affected (<a href="#s2-2">§2.2</a>). Four considerations support it: authorship, dependence, non-imposition, remedial. Defeaters D1–D4 are stated in <a href="#s2-5">§2.5</a>.</dd>

          <dt>Answerability</dt>
          <dd>The duty to give reasons for conditions one helps create or maintain, to permit examination, and to remain open to correction. Under the epistemic burden principle, the actor bears a heightened burden to test its account against independent evidence and meaningful correction under conditions of asymmetric influence (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>).</dd>

          <dt>Abductive support</dt>
          <dd>The form of argument used for AIP: it is a plausible explanatory framework of several considerations about the risks of unchecked power. Distinct from a deductive proof, a transcendental argument, or a convergence argument.</dd>

          <dt>Collective contestation</dt>
          <dd>Contestation by a community or people in their own right. Legitimate only if it does not undermine individual contestation within the collective (§4.6 vii).</dd>

          <dt>Contestation deficit (C)</dt>
          <dd>How hard it is for affected parties to exit, object effectively or find out what is done to them. The operational measure of how thoroughly exit and review fail, and therefore of how strongly justification is required (<a href="#s7">§7</a>).</dd>

          <dt>Counter-profile</dt>
          <dd>An alternative influence profile filed by an affected party, to which the actor must respond (<a href="#s9-4">§9.4</a>).</dd>

          <dt>Credible standing</dt>
          <dd>Evidence-based reason to think an entity has interests of its own (<a href="#s4-2">§4.2</a>).</dd>

          <dt>Defeaters</dt>
          <dd>The conditions under which AIP or the epistemic burden principle should be rejected or substantially revised. Stated in advance for AIP (D1–D4, <a href="#s2-5">§2.5</a>) and for the epistemic burden principle (B1–B5, <a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>).</dd>

          <dt>Discretion (D)</dt>
          <dd>Genuine alternatives and a role in setting the influence. The gate of the activation rule.</dd>

          <dt>Entrenchment (E)</dt>
          <dd>How long consequences last and how hard they are to undo.</dd>

          <dt>Epistemic burden principle (EBP)</dt>
          <dd>The claim that asymmetric influence creates a heightened burden to test the powerful actor's account against independent evidence and meaningful correction (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>). Defended against charges of circularity, overreach and metaphysical overreach, and against five stated defeaters (B1–B5). Limited to conditions of asymmetric influence; proceduralist rather than metaphysical.</dd>

          <dt>Exercised-Capacity Principle (ECP)</dt>
          <dd>Responsibility should be assessed in proportion to an actor's actual discretionary contribution to another party's conditions, taking account of consequences, foreseeability, alternatives, role and capacity to respond (<a href="#s3">§3</a>). A proposed allocation tool, not a replacement for Young's social connection model.</dd>

          <dt>Failure conditions (T1–T5)</dt>
          <dd>Five conditions under which the activation rule should be revised or abandoned (<a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a>). A methodological constraint, not a claim to have solved the philosophy of method. Requires preregistration.</dd>

          <dt>Finality without infallibility</dt>
          <dd>Decisions final for practical purposes but reopenable on defined grounds (<a href="#s11-2">§11.2</a>).</dd>

          <dt>The Floor (F1–F5)</dt>
          <dd>Minimum conditions in relationships of dependence: exit or voice, contestation, development, non-foreclosure, recognition (<a href="#s10">§10</a>).</dd>

          <dt>Levels 1–3</dt>
          <dd>Explain; review and respond (with safeguard analysis); preserve, examine and authorise (with safeguard mix) (<a href="#s8-2">§8.2</a>).</dd>

          <dt>Power objection</dt>
          <dd>The charge that proceduralism is itself a form of power, and that the framework's "handling" of domination is a way of legitimating it. Treated in <a href="#s2-6">§2.6</a> and objection 3.</dd>

          <dt>Presumptive level</dt>
          <dd>The level the activation rule yields, overridable only with stated reasons (<a href="#s7-3">§7.3</a>, <a href="#s9-3">§9.3</a>).</dd>

          <dt>Preregistration</dt>
          <dd>The requirement that the calibration set, raters, outcome measures, thresholds and analytic plan be fixed and made public before the tests in <a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a> are run. A test that is not preregistered should not be treated as a confirmatory test of the stated thresholds.</dd>

          <dt>Relational existence</dt>
          <dd>The empirical and phenomenological claim that no being exists except through relations of dependence, influence and constitution (<a href="#s5">§5</a>).</dd>

          <dt>Residual loss</dt>
          <dd>What must be acknowledged when no available option avoids serious harm. A defensible decision identifies whose interests were not met, why the competing claim prevailed, and what mitigation or restoration remains possible (<a href="#s12-2-6">§12.2.6</a>). A decision can be justified without being harmless.</dd>

          <dt>Safeguard analysis / safeguard mix</dt>
          <dd>The requirement at Level 2 that the actor identify which of exit, review or justification is most deficient in this relationship and which remedies address that specific deficit; and at Level 3 that the actor specify explicitly which safeguard mix the relationship needs, what each protects, and where each is weakest (<a href="#s8-2">§8.2</a>). The three safeguards are complementary, not interchangeable.</dd>

          <dt>Scope conditions</dt>
          <dd>The conditions under which the framework does not apply: symmetrical relations, relations governed by enforceable rights, purely causal influence without discretion, trivial influence, relations where the affected party is not a candidate for standing (<a href="#s1-3">§1.3</a>).</dd>

          <dt>Severity (S)</dt>
          <dd>How much is at stake for the affected, rated at individual and, for system design, aggregate level.</dd>

          <dt>Symbiotic standing</dt>
          <dd>A normative ideal: relations of dependence in which the dependence is not exploited, difference is not forced into sameness, and the dependent party keeps the conditions for continued existence, development and participation (<a href="#s5">§5</a>). Made measurable through S1–S3 (<a href="#s5-1">§5.1</a>). Under the epistemic burden principle, answerability is its heightened epistemic safeguard under asymmetric influence (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>).</dd>

          <dt>Symbiosis dimensions (S1–S3)</dt>
          <dd>Non-exploitation, non-assimilation, continued possibility (<a href="#s5-1">§5.1</a>). Conceptually distinct from answerability, though measurement may rely on contestable testimony and independent evidence.</dd>

          <dt>Three questions (Responsibility / Answerability / Justice)</dt>
          <dd>The framework's structuring distinction (<a href="#s1-2">§1.2</a>). Responsibility asks who shaped the conditions; Answerability asks who must explain and submit to correction; Justice asks what is substantively permissible. The framework answers the first two and leaves the third to substantive theories of justice.</dd>

          <dt>Tiers A–D</dt>
          <dd>Agents; welfare subjects; uncertain candidates; non-subjects (<a href="#s4-1">§4.1</a>).</dd>
        </dl>
      </section>

