      <section id="discussion">
        <h2>Discussion and contact</h2>
        <p>This essay is offered as a framework to be argued with. Two channels are open for that purpose, and a third — the comment thread below — for anything that fits neither.</p>

        <div class="note">
          <strong>Call for comments.</strong> Eleven specific areas of criticism are invited in particular:
          <ol style="margin-top:.6rem;margin-bottom:0">
            <li><strong>The four considerations (<a href="#s2-1">§2.1</a>)</strong> — do authorship, dependence, non-imposition and remedial responsibility genuinely support AIP, or are they one tradition dressed in four vocabularies?</li>
            <li><strong>The defeaters for AIP (<a href="#s2-5">§2.5</a>)</strong> — are D1–D4 genuine defeaters, or are they toothless?</li>
            <li><strong>The abductive support (<a href="#s2-4">§2.4</a>)</strong> — is AIP a plausible and useful explanation of the risks of unchecked power, or do rival principles explain them better?</li>
            <li><strong>The power objection (<a href="#s2-6">§2.6</a>)</strong> — does the framework's proceduralism simply reproduce the power it diagnoses? Is T5-as-precondition enough, or is it a relocation of the problem?</li>
            <li><strong>ECP against Young (<a href="#s3-2">§3.2</a>)</strong> — does the discretionary trigger really add what the social connection model lacks?</li>
            <li><strong>Collective versus individual contestation (<a href="#s4-6">§4.6</a>)</strong> — does condition (vii) really protect individuals within collectives?</li>
            <li><strong>Making symbiotic standing measurable (<a href="#s5-1">§5.1</a>)</strong> — are S1–S3 the right dimensions, and are they really independent of answerability?</li>
            <li><strong>The epistemic burden principle (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>)</strong> — is the argument circular? Are S1–S3 really independently specified? Does asymmetric influence justify a heightened burden of independent evidence? Are affected-party reports given appropriate weight without being romanticised? Are the five defeaters (B1–B5) adequate?</li>
            <li><strong>The safeguard analysis/mix (<a href="#s8-2">§8.2</a>)</strong> — does the shift from disjunctive to complementary safeguards strengthen the framework, or does it just relocate the same problem?</li>
            <li><strong>The residual loss requirement (<a href="#s12-2-6">§12.2.6</a>)</strong> — is it operational, or is it a rhetorical gesture?</li>
            <li><strong>The preregistration requirement (<a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a>)</strong> — is it operational, or does it merely look operational?</li>
          </ol>
          <p style="margin-top:.6rem;margin-bottom:0">Substantive objections that are not addressed will be listed in a future revision. The framework is offered as a tool, and its proposed epistemic principle is open to defeat. Its success will be measured by whether it is used, tested and replaced — and whether the epistemic burden principle survives the objections it invites.</p>
