      <section id="about">
        <h2>About this text</h2>
        <p><em>By Jesper Blom.</em></p>
        <p>This is an independent philosophical essay. It has not been peer reviewed, and it does not claim the authority of an established tradition. It is offered as a framework — a set of distinctions that may be useful for reasoning about responsibility in contexts where capability is growing faster than accountability. Its contribution is comparative and partly philosophical.</p>
        <p>The text is deliberately self-contained in its argument, though it engages with the work of others (see Bibliography). Readers who wish to test the framework are invited to apply it to cases the essay does not discuss, and to look for the places where it strains.</p>
        <p>Seven features of the framework deserve comment, in order of the stress-test that produced them.</p>
        <p>First, the framework distinguishes three questions that must not be collapsed: responsibility, answerability, and justice (<a href="#s1-2">§1.2</a>). Second, the central principle is AIP, a proportionate principle supported by four considerations (<a href="#s2-2">§2.2</a>), with stated defeaters D1–D4 (<a href="#s2-5">§2.5</a>). Third, the three safeguards — exit, review, justification — are complementary, not interchangeable (<a href="#s8-1">§8.1</a>). Fourth, collective contestation may represent silent parties, but it must not undermine individual contestation (<a href="#s4-6">§4.6</a>). Fifth, the framework handles domination; it does not solve it (<a href="#s3-2">§3.2</a>, <a href="#s16">§16</a>), and the power objection (<a href="#s2-6">§2.6</a>) is the point at which this limit presses hardest. Sixth, conflicts between legitimate interests are handled through a six-step priority structure that includes residual loss (<a href="#s12-2">§12.2</a>).</p>
        <p>Seventh, and most importantly, one proposed contribution is the <em>epistemic burden principle</em> (<a href="#s5-2">§5.2</a>). The principle argues that asymmetric influence can shape both the decision and the evidence used to assess it. The proposed response is a proportionate burden to seek independent scrutiny and meaningful correction from affected parties or legitimate representatives. It is not claimed as a necessary condition of knowledge, nor as a gap that the major traditions have simply overlooked. Its value depends on whether it improves the allocation and design of answerability in concrete cases. The argument has five defeaters (B1–B5) and remains open to counterexample.</p>
        <p>The framework's methodological constraint (<a href="#s2-5">§2.5</a>, <a href="#s15-2">§15.2</a>) remains: it states, in advance, four defeaters for the fallibilist premise (<a href="#s2-0">§2.0</a>), four defeaters for AIP, five defeaters for the epistemic burden principle, and five proposed evaluation conditions for its activation rule, and it requires preregistration for the tests. But the constraint applies to the activation rule and to the ground, not to every part of the framework.</p>

        <div class="note"><strong>Corrections and criticism</strong> are welcome. A framework that cannot survive disagreement was never a framework to begin with. A framework that cannot be replaced cannot be corrected — see <a href="#s15-3">§15.3</a>.</div>

